###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AcquisitionContext
ontoksd:AcquisitionContext rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Context ;
                           rdfs:comment """A contextual configuration of educational circumstances relevant to planned instruction or the realization and interpretation of learning, training, or assessment.

In OntoKSD, AcquisitionContext specializes Context and, consequently, gufo:Situation. It represents the relevant configuration of conditions rather than an instructional specification, an execution event, a physical environment, or a learner's acquisition of competence.

An AcquisitionContext may describe learning modality, available resources, collaboration arrangements, institutional conditions, pedagogical support, temporal boundaries, and assessment circumstances. Its description should identify the conditions relevant to the instructional unit or occurrence concerned, rather than merely naming a classroom, platform, or activity.

An InstructionalUnit may identify its anticipated educational circumstances through hasAcquisitionContext, whose inverse is isAcquisitionContextOf. An AssessmentOccurrence may identify its recorded circumstances through occursInAcquisitionContext, whose inverse is isContextOfAssessmentOccurrence. Anticipated and recorded conditions need not coincide and must remain distinguishable.

AcquisitionContext is distinct from LearningActivity and LearningTask, which specify planned learner engagement and work, and from AssessmentOccurrence, which represents a concrete evaluative event. It also differs from ActivationConstraint, which specifies an applicable condition of a competence–instructional unit association. Such a condition may contribute to the contextual configuration without describing it completely.

Examples include the configuration of laboratory resources and pair-work arrangements for automata modeling, the tool availability and time limitations of an online simulation, or the collaboration and mentoring conditions of a project.

The context may be shared by multiple specifications or occurrences when its meaning and boundaries genuinely apply to each. Relevant changes should be explicitly represented rather than silently modifying the circumstances used to interpret existing records.

The term acquisition identifies the educational scope of the context. Its representation does not establish that learning occurred, that competence was acquired, that an instructional condition was satisfied, or that a demonstration transfers to other contexts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationConstraint
ontoksd:ActivationConstraint rdf:type owl:Class ;
                             rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ,
                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasActivationConstraintType ;
                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:ActivationConstraintType
                                             ] ,
                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasActivationConstraintDescription ;
                                               owl:someValuesFrom rdf:langString
                                             ] ;
                             rdfs:comment """An activation qualifier that specifies a condition, limitation, or requirement applicable to the expected mobilization of a competence within an instructional unit.

In OntoKSD, ActivationConstraint is a subclass of ActivationQualifier associated with CompetenceActivation through hasActivationConstraint. It records a condition of the competence–instructional unit association rather than an intrinsic restriction on the reusable Competence definition.

A constraint may concern tools, permitted representations, time limits, collaboration arrangements, available resources, or conditions of demonstration or assessment. Examples include using a designated simulator, justifying modeling decisions, completing work individually, or satisfying a specified resource limitation.

Each ActivationConstraint has an explicit description through hasActivationConstraintDescription and a classification through hasActivationConstraintType. The description identifies the condition itself, while the type indicates its status, such as mandatory, optional, or conditional. A status value alone does not specify a condition. For a conditional constraint, the description should also explain when it applies.

ActivationConstraint is distinct from ActivationFunction, which identifies the pedagogical purpose of the association; ActivationRole, which identifies the competence's contribution; and ActivationMode, which identifies the expected form of engagement.

The constraint qualifies how a competence is addressed without silently changing its specified performance or constituent requirements. If an instructional demand exceeds or changes the competence definition, that difference must be examined separately rather than represented solely as an activation constraint.

Requirements applying to an instructional unit as a whole must remain recordable independently of competence associations. ActivationConstraint does not replace the complete description of a LearningTask, including requirements for which no CompetenceActivation has been asserted.

A constraint specification may be reused across activations where its content and intended applicability remain unchanged. It is not modeled as an individual quality inhering in a single activation.

ActivationConstraint belongs to the instructional specification layer. Recording a constraint does not establish that it has been satisfied, that the specified work has occurred, or that a learner has demonstrated competence. Any verification of compliance requires separately recorded information and an explicit evaluation procedure.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationConstraintType
ontoksd:ActivationConstraintType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                 rdfs:comment """A classificatory category that identifies the status of a condition specified by an ActivationConstraint.

In OntoKSD, instances of ActivationConstraintType are reusable controlled values associated with ActivationConstraint through hasActivationConstraintType. Supported distinctions include mandatory, optional, and conditional.

Mandatory indicates that the described condition must be satisfied under its stated applicability. Optional indicates that a described provision or alternative is permitted but not required. Conditional indicates that the condition becomes applicable when an explicitly stated circumstance holds; its description should explain both the triggering circumstance and what is then expected.

These values classify a condition rather than specify its content. For example, mandatory does not identify which tool must be used, which action must be performed, or which limitation applies. That information is recorded through hasActivationConstraintDescription.

The controlled values should have distinct, documented meanings. In the current single-type classification, a condition that is mandatory only under particular circumstances may be classified as conditional, with its description stating the trigger and the resulting obligation. The type alone does not encode a complete rule for evaluating compliance.

ActivationConstraintType values may be shared across multiple constraint specifications. They are classifications rather than individual qualities inhering in a single constraint or activation.

Assigning a type records the intended status of a condition. It does not establish that the condition applies to a particular execution, has been satisfied, or has been checked automatically.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationFunction
ontoksd:ActivationFunction rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:comment """A classificatory category that identifies the pedagogical purpose of a competence–instructional unit association.

In OntoKSD, instances of ActivationFunction are reusable controlled values associated with CompetenceActivation through hasActivationFunction. They describe why a competence is included in the instructional specification, without modifying its reusable definition or asserting that learners have demonstrated it.

Supported distinctions may include a competence being addressed as an instructional target or presupposed as a prerequisite. targetFunction identifies a competence that the instructional unit is intended to develop, practice, or provide opportunities to demonstrate. Where prerequisiteFunction is used, it identifies a competence presupposed for engagement in the instructional unit; it does not establish that any particular learner satisfies that prerequisite.

These functions qualify the association between a Competence and an InstructionalUnit. They do not express a prerequisite relationship between two competence specifications or establish a curricular expectation at the Course level.

ActivationFunction is distinct from ActivationRole, ActivationMode, and ActivationConstraint. Function identifies the pedagogical purpose of the association; Role identifies the competence's contribution within the instructional unit, such as central or supporting; Mode identifies the expected form of engagement; and Constraint specifies an applicable condition. A competence may therefore be an instructional target while playing a supporting role and being addressed through analytical engagement.

Function values are shared classifications rather than individual qualities inhering in a single activation. The same value may qualify multiple CompetenceActivation instances without identifying those associations or their instructional units as the same entity.

The vocabulary should reflect distinctions explicitly used in the instructional specifications. Additional functions may be introduced when their meanings are clearly defined and distinguishable from roles, modes, and conditions. Multiple function assignments require an interpretation consistent with the intended instructional association.

ActivationFunction belongs to the instructional specification layer. Its assignment records an educational intention or presupposition, not an occurrence of performance, evidence of attainment, or confirmation that the instructional purpose has been fulfilled.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationMode
ontoksd:ActivationMode rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                       rdfs:comment """An activation qualifier that classifies the expected form of engagement through which a competence is addressed within an instructional unit.

In OntoKSD, ActivationMode is a subclass of ActivationQualifier. Its instances are reusable controlled values associated with CompetenceActivation through hasActivationMode. They characterize the anticipated cognitive or operational engagement, as expressed in the instructional design and expected learner actions.

Examples include constructive engagement through producing or modifying a solution; analytical engagement through examining structures or behavior; interpretative engagement through explaining meaning; and justificatory engagement through defending decisions using reasons, evidence, or criteria. Each mode should have an explicit definition that guides its consistent application.

Modes are not mutually exclusive. A single CompetenceActivation may include several modes when the planned work requires different forms of engagement, such as constructing a model, analyzing its behavior, and justifying its design. Multiple mode assignments do not, by themselves, establish an execution sequence or identify separate phases of work.

ActivationMode is distinct from Skill and SkillLevel. Skill specifies a capability within a competence definition, while SkillLevel qualifies required performance in the applicable reference scheme. ActivationMode classifies how that competence is pedagogically addressed in a particular association. A mode label does not, by itself, determine a taxonomic level or change the level required by the competence specification.

ActivationMode also differs from ActivationFunction, which identifies the pedagogical purpose of the association; ActivationRole, which identifies the competence's contribution; and ActivationConstraint, which specifies an applicable condition.

The same mode value may qualify multiple activations, and the same competence may be addressed through different modes across instructional units. Such variation must remain consistent with the competence definition. A change of mode does not automatically establish specialization, composition, or an extension of the competence's performance scope.

ActivationMode values are shared classifications rather than individual qualities inhering in a single activation. Their reuse does not identify the associated activations or instructional units as the same entity.

ActivationMode belongs to the instructional specification layer. Its assignment describes expected engagement rather than an observed cognitive process, completed action, or evidence of learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationQualifier
ontoksd:ActivationQualifier rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:comment """A contextual qualification element that characterizes how a competence is expected to contribute to, be engaged with, or be subject to conditions within an instructional unit.

In OntoKSD, ActivationQualifier provides a common superclass for ActivationRole, ActivationMode, and ActivationConstraint. These subclasses qualify a CompetenceActivation while retaining distinct meanings:

• ActivationRole identifies the competence's contribution within the instructional unit, such as central, supporting, or transversal.

• ActivationMode identifies the expected form of engagement, such as constructive, analytical, interpretative, or justificatory.

• ActivationConstraint specifies an applicable condition, whose content and classification are represented separately.

ActivationQualifier supports uniform association and retrieval through hasActivationQualifier and its specialized properties. It is a structural abstraction for qualification elements, rather than an assertion that all subclasses represent the same kind of information. Roles and modes may use shared controlled values, whereas constraints identify explicitly described conditions.

ActivationFunction is represented separately. It identifies the pedagogical purpose of the competence–instructional unit association, rather than its contribution, form of engagement, or applicable conditions.

A CompetenceActivation may have multiple qualifiers. Their assignment describes the instructional association without modifying the reusable Competence specification. Changes in qualification must not silently introduce changes to the competence's expected performance or constituent requirements.

ActivationQualifier is not modeled as an individual quality with a single bearer. Shared qualifier values may be associated with multiple activations without identifying those activations as the same entity. A constraint may be reused where its content and intended applicability remain unchanged.

The class is intended to be specialized rather than used as an uninformative generic classification. It remains extensible to additional qualification types when their meanings and purposes are explicitly justified.

ActivationQualifier belongs to the instructional specification layer. Its associations express planned contributions, engagement, and conditions; they do not establish that instruction has occurred, that a condition has been satisfied, or that a learner has demonstrated competence.""" ;
                            skos:example """# Competência de referência para o exemplo.
# Não recebe um padrão específico, pois seus constituintes
# não são detalhados neste recorte.

ex:C01 a owl:NamedIndividual, ontoksd:Competence ;
    rdfs:label
        \"Desenvolver soluções usando autômatos finitos\"@pt .

# Especificação instrucional, distinta da Task da competência.

ex:tarefaAutomatos
    a owl:NamedIndividual, ontoksd:LearningTask ;
    rdfs:label
        \"Construção e análise de um autômato finito\"@pt ;
    rdfs:comment
        \"\"\"Construir um autômato finito para a linguagem indicada
        no enunciado, testar seu comportamento e explicar os
        resultados obtidos.\"\"\"@pt .

# Condição identificável, com conteúdo e classificação.

ex:constraintSimulador
    a owl:NamedIndividual, ontoksd:ActivationConstraint ;
    ontoksd:hasActivationConstraintType
        ontoksd:acMandatory ;
    ontoksd:hasActivationConstraintDescription
        \"\"\"Utilizar o simulador indicado no enunciado da atividade
        para construir o autômato e testar a aceitação ou rejeição
        das cadeias fornecidas.\"\"\"@pt .

# Associação instrucional qualificada.

ex:ativacao1
    a owl:NamedIndividual, ontoksd:CompetenceActivation ;
    ontoksd:mediatesCompetence ex:C01 ;
    ontoksd:mediatesInstructionalUnit ex:tarefaAutomatos ;
    ontoksd:hasActivationFunction ontoksd:targetFunction ;
    ontoksd:hasActivationRole ontoksd:arCore ;
    ontoksd:hasActivationMode
        ontoksd:amConstructive,
        ontoksd:amAnalytical ;
    ontoksd:hasActivationConstraint ex:constraintSimulador .""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationRole
ontoksd:ActivationRole rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                       rdfs:comment """An activation qualifier that classifies the contribution of a competence within a particular instructional unit.

In OntoKSD, ActivationRole is a subclass of ActivationQualifier. Its instances are reusable controlled values associated with CompetenceActivation through hasActivationRole. They indicate the position or contribution assigned to the competence in the instructional design, rather than an intrinsic characteristic of the competence.

Examples include a central contribution, where the competence is a principal focus of the planned work; a supporting contribution, where it enables or assists other expected performance; and a transversal contribution, where it supports engagement across aspects of the instructional work without necessarily being its principal focus. Each role value should have an explicit definition that guides its consistent assignment.

ActivationRole is distinct from ActivationFunction. Function identifies the pedagogical purpose of the association, such as treating the competence as an instructional target or presupposing it as a prerequisite. Role identifies its contribution within that association. A supporting role does not, by itself, establish a prerequisite, and a central role does not automatically establish that the competence is assessed.

ActivationRole also differs from ActivationMode, which classifies the expected form of engagement, and ActivationConstraint, which specifies an applicable condition.

The same competence may have different roles in different instructional units without changing its reusable definition. A role assignment does not establish composition, specialization, prerequisite relationships, or dependencies between competence specifications.

Role values are shared classifications rather than individual qualities inhering in a single activation. The same value may therefore qualify multiple CompetenceActivation instances without identifying those associations as the same entity. Multiple roles may be assigned where their definitions and the instructional description support a coherent interpretation.

A transversal role describes the contribution intended in the association concerned. Demonstrating that a competence is addressed across several instructional units requires examining the corresponding associations; it does not follow from a single role assignment.

ActivationRole belongs to the instructional specification layer. It describes an intended contribution rather than actual learner participation, observed performance, or evidence that the competence has been attained.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AlignmentType
ontoksd:AlignmentType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:comment """A classificatory category that identifies the principal nature of a correspondence asserted between an internal competence specification and an external competence reference.

In OntoKSD, instances of AlignmentType are reusable controlled values associated with ExternalCompetenceAlignment through hasAlignmentType. They distinguish interpretations such as equivalent, close, partial, broader, narrower, contextual, or curricular-continuity correspondence.

Directional types are interpreted from the internal Competence toward the ExternalCompetence. BroaderThanExternalAlignment means that the internal specification has broader semantic coverage; NarrowerThanExternalAlignment means that its scope is more restricted.

Each alignment has exactly one principal type under the adopted model. Because some values characterize semantic coverage while others characterize curricular relationships, the selection policy should be explicit. Additional qualifications and scope restrictions are recorded in the alignment justification.

A type identifies the asserted interpretation but does not replace its supporting explanation or source. In particular, a partial alignment should identify both the corresponding aspects and the relevant limits of coverage.

AlignmentType values do not automatically establish logical equivalence, identity, subsumption, competence attainment, or regulatory compliance. EquivalentAlignment records an interpreted correspondence rather than asserting owl:sameAs or owl:equivalentClass."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Assessment
ontoksd:Assessment rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:InstructionalUnit ;
                   owl:disjointWith ontoksd:AssessmentOccurrence ,
                                    ontoksd:AssessmentParticipation ;
                   rdfs:comment """An instructional unit that specifies how learner performance, products, or observations are to be evaluated in relation to stated expectations and criteria.

In OntoKSD, Assessment is a subclass of InstructionalUnit representing evaluative design. It describes the planned basis for judgment rather than an evaluation event, an individual result, or evidence that a competence has been demonstrated.

An Assessment may specify expected actions, products, or observations, applicable criteria, supporting resources, and scoring parameters. These expectations must remain distinguishable from collected evidence and recorded judgments. Where a composite competence is addressed, the assessment description should identify any expectation concerning the integration of its components rather than assuming that separate component demonstrations establish integrated performance.

Competence associations are represented through CompetenceActivation. They identify the competences the assessment is intended to address and qualify their instructional use. An Assessment may also retain requirements and criteria for which no competence association has been asserted.

An Assessment may be based on a LearningActivity through isBasedOnActivity and use EducationalResource instances through usesResource, including rubrics, instructions, datasets, or tools. The activity, assessment specification, and supporting resources remain separately identifiable. Applicable criteria and their correspondence to competence requirements should be recoverable from the recorded descriptions or referenced resources.

Concrete realizations are represented by AssessmentOccurrence and linked through isRealizedBy. The same specification may be carried out on different occasions, each with its own participants, contextual conditions, and evidence. An Assessment need not have been executed to be represented.

AssessmentParticipation records individual involvement and results within an occurrence. Consequently, shared assessment design or a shared artefact does not imply identical results or evidential grounds for all learners.

An Assessment may be classified through hasAssessmentTypeConcept using controlled concepts whose schemes identify the relevant dimension, such as assessment agent, modality, or strategy. Where numeric scoring is used, maxScore, passingScore, and weight describe scoring parameters; they do not automatically calculate results or establish proficiency or competence attainment.

Examples include the specification of a written examination, programming test, oral evaluation, project assessment, or rubric-based review of a design report.

Assessment is distinct from LearningActivity, which specifies learning-oriented engagement, and from AssessmentOccurrence and AssessmentParticipation, which represent execution and individualized participation. This separation preserves the distinction between evaluative intentions, concrete assessment occasions, collected evidence, and learner-specific judgments.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentOccurrence
ontoksd:AssessmentOccurrence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                             rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Event> ,
                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:realizes ;
                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:Assessment
                                             ] ;
                             rdfs:comment """A concrete evaluative event that carries out an Assessment specification on a particular occasion.

In OntoKSD, AssessmentOccurrence is a subclass of gufo:Event and realizes exactly one Assessment through realizes. Assessment represents the evaluative design; AssessmentOccurrence represents its execution. The same assessment specification may have multiple occurrences, each independently identifiable by its occasion, temporal boundaries, participants, and relevant circumstances.

An AssessmentOccurrence may be situated in an AcquisitionContext through occursInAcquisitionContext. The occurrence identifies what happens, whereas the context describes the educational conditions under which it happens. Conditions recorded for an occurrence may differ from those anticipated by the instructional specification and should not silently replace them.

Individual participation is represented through AssessmentParticipation and linked by involvesAssessmentParticipation. Multiple learners may participate in the same occurrence while retaining separate participation records, scores, and competence-related judgments. The occurrence itself does not represent an individual learner's result.

Evidence produced during the event may be associated through producesEvidence. Such evidence may be individual or collective, but its association with the occurrence does not automatically make it the evidential basis for every participant's judgment. When an evaluation examines an artefact produced earlier, the artefact's original production must remain distinguishable from its use in the assessment.

The occurrence may identify an applicable ProficiencyScheme through usesProficiencyScheme. This association documents an interpretive framework; it does not automatically classify scores or assign proficiency levels. Where different judgments use different schemes, their applicability must remain explicit.

The occurrence's date or temporal interval should be recorded when needed to distinguish assessment occasions. These temporal references are distinct from dates of evidence production, judgment recording, or validation.

Examples include a particular administration of a written examination, a programming test conducted in a laboratory session, an oral evaluation, or the presentation and assessment of a group project.

AssessmentOccurrence is distinct from Assessment, AssessmentParticipation, and Context. Its representation establishes the assessment occasion but does not, by itself, establish that every participant completed the work, satisfied the assessment criteria, or demonstrated the competences addressed.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentParticipation
ontoksd:AssessmentParticipation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasLearner ;
                                                  owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                  owl:onClass ontoksd:Learner
                                                ] ,
                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:occursInAssessmentOccurrence ;
                                                  owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                  owl:onClass ontoksd:AssessmentOccurrence
                                                ] ,
                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasScore ;
                                                  owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                  owl:onDataRange xsd:double
                                                ] ;
                                rdfs:comment """An individualized assessment record that identifies a learner's participation in a particular assessment occurrence and provides an anchor for participant-specific results and competence-related judgments.

In OntoKSD, AssessmentParticipation connects exactly one Learner through hasLearner with exactly one AssessmentOccurrence through occursInAssessmentOccurrence. It represents the participant-specific record rather than the assessment specification, the evaluative event itself, or a judgment that a competence has been attained.

An AssessmentParticipation may identify one or more assessors through hasAssessor. This association records their involvement in the participant-specific evaluation; it does not, by itself, establish that a particular judgment has been issued.

The class supports individual and group-based assessment. Multiple learners may participate in the same occurrence and share an artefact while having separate participation records. Their scores, supporting evidence, and competence-related judgments may differ. Where the same group score is assigned to several learners, each assignment remains individually recorded rather than inferred solely from shared participation.

An AssessmentParticipation may record at most one overall numeric result through hasScore. The score is distinct from a proficiency interpretation and does not necessarily express achievement of every competence addressed by the assessment. Criterion-specific or competence-specific results require separately identifiable records when such granularity is needed.

EvidencedCompetence and EvidencedCompetenceConstituent records may refer to the participation through derivedFromParticipation. These records identify the competence or constituent concerned and the evidence supporting the individual interpretation. The participation provides the learner and occasion to which those records must remain consistent; it does not replace their evidential justification.

Evidence associated with the occurrence is not automatically attributed to every participant, and a shared artefact does not establish an identical evidential basis for individual judgments. The evidence actually supporting each judgment must remain identifiable.

Different assessment occasions require distinct participation records, even when the learner and assessment specification are unchanged. The learner–occurrence association does not, by itself, define a uniqueness key for participation records; any additional individuation policy must be explicitly documented.

AssessmentParticipation is modeled as a contextualized record without asserting gUFO relator semantics. Its association with an AssessmentOccurrence links the record to an event and is not represented as mediation between endurants.

The class preserves the distinction between assessment design, concrete execution, individual participation, numeric results, supporting evidence, and competence-related judgments. Recording participation alone does not establish completion, a passing result, or competence attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Assessor
ontoksd:Assessor rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Person .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AtomicCompetence
ontoksd:AtomicCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:Competence
                                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasStructureType ;
                                                                      owl:hasValue ontoksd:Atomic
                                                                    ]
                                                                  ) ;
                                               rdf:type owl:Class
                                             ] ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Competence ,
                                         [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                           owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasComponentCompetence ;
                                           owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "0"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                           owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                         ] ;
                         owl:disjointWith ontoksd:CompositeCompetence ;
                         rdfs:comment """AtomicCompetence

A competence specification represented as a single structural unit without component competences.

In OntoKSD, AtomicCompetence is a subclass of Competence associated with the StructureType value Atomic. Its structural restriction excludes component competences through hasComponentCompetence.

Atomicity concerns decomposition into other competence specifications. It does not exclude internal constituents such as Knowledge, Skill, Disposition, Attitude, or KnowledgeSkill, nor does it exclude an associated Task. An atomic competence may consequently have a detailed semantic specification while remaining structurally undecomposed at the competence-to-competence level.

Classification as AtomicCompetence is an explicit modeling commitment within the scope and granularity of the represented specification. It must not be inferred merely because component competences have not yet been recorded. If a subsequent revision introduces component competences, its atomic classification must be reconsidered.

AtomicCompetence is disjoint with CompositeCompetence. Atomicity does not prohibit specialization relationships, prerequisite relationships, or participation as a component of another competence.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Attitude
ontoksd:Attitude rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Disposition ;
                 rdfs:comment """A disposition constituent that specifies an evaluative orientation toward an object, practice, behavior, or situation relevant to expected competent performance.

In OntoKSD, Attitude represents an attitudinal requirement within a competence specification. Its distinguishing feature is an evaluative position, such as approval, disapproval, acceptance, or regard, directed toward a relevant target. Examples include a favorable orientation toward peer review or an orientation of respect toward users' perspectives.

Attitude is modeled as a subclass of Disposition because, within the adopted competence-specification scope, such evaluative orientations are treated as dispositional elements expected to guide the mobilization of knowledge and skills. This subsumption is a modeling commitment supported by the broad treatment of dispositions in CC2020 and CS2023; it is not a claim that every use of the term attitude across the literature has the same meaning.

Every Attitude is therefore a Disposition in OntoKSD, but classification as Disposition does not imply classification as Attitude. The distinction preserves the specific attitudinal requirement of the KSA specification pattern while allowing attitudinal constituents to participate in general operations over dispositional constituents.

An Attitude may be associated with a Competence through hasAttitude. Because hasAttitude specializes hasDisposition, this association also identifies the element as a disposition of the competence. A generic Disposition does not, by itself, satisfy a requirement for an Attitude constituent.

Attitude is distinct from Skill and from an occurrence of behavior. A favorable orientation toward a practice does not establish the capability to perform it, and performing an action does not necessarily establish the corresponding evaluative orientation. The distinction between Attitude and Disposition is not based on an assumption that attitudes are temporary whereas dispositions are permanent.

Attitude belongs to the specification layer. Its instances describe expected attitudinal orientations rather than an individual's measured attitude, verbal response, or observed behavior. Learner-specific evidence and its interpretation are represented separately through EvidencedCompetenceConstituent and associated evidence and assessment relations.

Elements imported from external KSA models should be classified as Attitude according to their definitions and intended meanings, rather than solely because their source uses the label attitude.""" ;
                 rdfs:label "Attitude"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ExternalCompetence ;
                       rdfs:comment """An ExternalCompetence that references a competence explicitly identified in the Base Nacional Comum Curricular (BNCC) or its applicable curricular complements.

In OntoKSD, BNCCCompetence preserves a source-defined curricular expectation as an external reference. Its record should identify the authoritative statement, source document, edition or version, identifier where available, and applicable educational stage or curricular scope.

Under the adopted class definition, BNCCCompetence comprises BNCCGeneralCompetence and BNCCSpecificCompetence, which are disjoint categories. Classification must follow the category and meaning established by the source rather than the apparent breadth of the statement.

A curricular item identified as a skill, learning objective, or knowledge topic is not automatically a BNCCCompetence. Such items require an explicit representation decision that preserves their source category.

BNCCCompetence does not automatically adopt the internal constituent structure or specification patterns of Competence. Where an internal specification addresses or operationalizes aspects of a BNCC expectation, the correspondence is documented through ExternalCompetenceAlignment, including its type, justification, and limits.

The class supports traceable comparison with national curricular expectations. Its representation or alignment does not, by itself, establish semantic equivalence, direct substitution, complete pedagogical coverage, regulatory compliance, or learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCGeneralCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCGeneralCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:BNCCCompetence ;
                              owl:disjointWith ontoksd:BNCCSpecificCompetence ;
                              rdfs:comment """A BNCCCompetence that references a statement explicitly identified by the BNCC as a General Competence of Basic Education.

In OntoKSD, BNCCGeneralCompetence represents a broad, transversal curricular expectation rather than a domain-specific competence specification. Classification follows the source category; a statement is not classified as general merely because its wording is broad or transferable.

Each instance should preserve the source statement and identify its official designation or number, source document, and relevant version. General competences may involve knowledge, capabilities, values, and attitudes, but are not thereby classified as Knowledge, Skill, Disposition, or Attitude constituents of OntoKSD.

An instance may exist independently of an alignment or serve as the external endpoint of multiple ExternalCompetenceAlignment records. Each alignment must explain the contribution or correspondence of the internal competence and its limits. Addressing one aspect of a general competence does not establish coverage of the entire expectation.

BNCCGeneralCompetence is disjoint with BNCCSpecificCompetence. Its use as an external reference does not automatically impose a specification pattern, taxonomic level, or constituent structure on an internal Competence.

The class supports traceability to overarching curricular expectations without asserting semantic equivalence, regulatory compliance, or learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCSpecificCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCSpecificCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                               rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:BNCCCompetence ;
                               rdfs:comment """A BNCCCompetence that references a statement explicitly identified as a Specific Competence in the BNCC or an applicable curricular complement.

In OntoKSD, BNCCSpecificCompetence preserves an externally defined educational expectation associated with an identifiable curricular scope, such as a knowledge area, curricular component, or educational stage. Classification follows the source category rather than the apparent specificity of its wording.

Each instance should identify the authoritative statement, designation or code where available, source document, relevant edition or version, and applicable curricular scope. Items designated as skills or knowledge topics are not automatically classified as specific competences.

The class does not require the external statement to adopt OntoKSD's internal constituent structure or specification patterns. Where an internal Competence addresses or operationalizes the external expectation, that correspondence is represented separately through ExternalCompetenceAlignment.

An alignment should identify the aspects addressed, its principal type, and relevant limits. Similar terminology, shared subject matter, or a common instructional activity does not establish complete correspondence.

BNCCSpecificCompetence is disjoint with BNCCGeneralCompetence. Its classification does not automatically establish a specialization relationship with a general competence or identify a required skill level.

The class supports traceability between internal specifications and source-defined curricular expectations. Its representation or alignment does not, by itself, establish complete coverage, direct substitution, regulatory compliance, or learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CC2020Competence
ontoksd:CC2020Competence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:Competence
                                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSpecificationPattern ;
                                                                      owl:hasValue ontoksd:CC2020SpecificationPattern
                                                                    ]
                                                                  ) ;
                                               rdf:type owl:Class
                                             ] ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Competence ,
                                         [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasDisposition ;
                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Disposition
                                                                ]
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasKnowledgeSkill ;
                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill
                                                                ]
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasTask ;
                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Task
                                                                ]
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasKnowledgeSkill ;
                                                                  owl:allValuesFrom [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill
                                                                                                           [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                                             owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSkillLevel ;
                                                                                                             owl:someValuesFrom <http://purl.org/edu/bloom#CC2020SkillLevel>
                                                                                                           ]
                                                                                                         ) ;
                                                                                      rdf:type owl:Class
                                                                                    ]
                                                                ]
                                                              ) ;
                                           rdf:type owl:Class
                                         ] ;
                         rdfs:comment """CC2020Competence

A competence specification that adopts the Knowledge–Skill–Disposition (KSD) pattern represented by CC2020SpecificationPattern.

In OntoKSD, CC2020Competence is defined as a Competence associated with CC2020SpecificationPattern through hasSpecificationPattern. Its minimum constituent structure includes at least one KnowledgeSkill pairing through hasKnowledgeSkill and at least one Disposition element through hasDisposition.

Each KnowledgeSkill pairing associates a Knowledge element with a required SkillLevel, making explicit the level of performance expected in relation to that knowledge. This representation preserves the association between knowledge and its required mobilization without treating a taxonomic performance level as an individuated Skill constituent.

Disposition identifies an orientation expected to shape competent performance, including attitudinal, value-based, belief-based, or other disposition-related elements within the scope of the ontology.

The reference task may be expressed in the competence statement without being represented as a separate Task individual. An explicit Task may nevertheless be associated with the competence.

CC2020Competence represents an expected performance specification. Its classification indicates adoption of the CC2020 specification pattern within OntoKSD and does not, by itself, establish external validation or evidence of individual attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CS2023Competence
ontoksd:CS2023Competence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:Competence
                                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSpecificationPattern ;
                                                                      owl:hasValue ontoksd:CS2023SpecificationPattern
                                                                    ]
                                                                  ) ;
                                               rdf:type owl:Class
                                             ] ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Competence ,
                                         [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasDisposition ;
                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Disposition
                                                                ]
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasKnowledgeSkill ;
                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill
                                                                ]
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasTask ;
                                                                  owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Task
                                                                ]
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasKnowledgeSkill ;
                                                                  owl:allValuesFrom [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill
                                                                                                           [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                                                             owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSkillLevel ;
                                                                                                             owl:someValuesFrom <http://purl.org/edu/cs2023#CS2023SkillLevel>
                                                                                                           ]
                                                                                                         ) ;
                                                                                      rdf:type owl:Class
                                                                                    ]
                                                                ]
                                                              ) ;
                                           rdf:type owl:Class
                                         ] ;
                         rdfs:comment """A competence specification that adopts the Knowledge–Skill–Disposition
plus Task (KSD-T) pattern represented by CS2023SpecificationPattern.

In OntoKSD, CS2023Competence is defined as a Competence associated with
CS2023SpecificationPattern through hasSpecificationPattern. Its minimum
structure includes at least one KnowledgeSkill pairing through
hasKnowledgeSkill, at least one Disposition element through
hasDisposition, and at least one explicit Task through hasTask.

Each KnowledgeSkill pairing associates a Knowledge element with a
required SkillLevel, identifying the expected level of performance in
relation to that knowledge. Disposition identifies an orientation
expected to shape competent performance.

Task makes explicit the reference performance addressed by the
competence. It is a specification-level construct associated directly
with Competence, rather than a CompetenceConstituent, an instructional
activity, or a concrete occurrence of performance. Its explicit
representation distinguishes the structural requirements of this
pattern from those of CC2020Competence.

The same CS2023Competence specification may be reused across different
instructional units, activation contexts, and assessments. Its
classification indicates adoption of the CS2023 specification pattern
within OntoKSD; learner-specific demonstrations and their supporting
evidence are represented separately.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Competence
ontoksd:Competence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:comment """A formal and reusable specification of expected competent performance in a given domain.

In OntoKSD, Competence represents what a learner or practitioner is expected to know, be able to do, and, where required by the adopted specification pattern, be disposed to demonstrate. It serves as a reference for instructional planning, assessment, and the interpretation of demonstrated performance. It represents an expected performance specification rather than an individual's actual capabilities, an occurrence of performance, or an evidential record.

The internal structure of a Competence depends on its SpecificationPattern. OntoKSD supports four patterns:

• KS: knowledge and skill constituents.

• KSA: knowledge, skill, and attitude constituents, with Attitude modeled as a specialization of Disposition.

• KSD, represented by CC2020SpecificationPattern: knowledge–skill pairings and disposition constituents. Each KnowledgeSkill pairing associates a Knowledge element with a required level of performance, preserving the distinction between a Skill constituent and a SkillLevel.

• KSD-T, represented by CC2020SpecificationPattern and CS2023SpecificationPattern: knowledge–skill pairings, disposition constituents, and an explicitly represented specification-level Task.

Knowledge, Skill, Disposition, and KnowledgeSkill are represented as distinct subclasses of CompetenceConstituent. Task is associated directly with Competence through hasTask and is not classified as a CompetenceConstituent. It describes the reference task addressed by the competence, rather than a particular instructional activity, performance occurrence, or acquisition context. An explicit Task is required by the KSD-T pattern; its absence does not, by itself, invalidate a specification following another pattern.

A Competence can be reused across instructional units, activation contexts, and assessments. Its pedagogical activation is represented separately through CompetenceActivation, while learner-specific evidential records are represented through EvidencedCompetence and related constituent-level records. This separation allows the same competence specification to support different educational situations without conflating expected performance with evidence of its demonstration..""" ;
                   rdfs:label "Competence"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceActivation
ontoksd:CompetenceActivation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                             rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ,
                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesCompetence ;
                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                             ] ,
                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesInstructionalUnit ;
                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:InstructionalUnit
                                             ] ;
                             rdfs:comment """A qualified instructional association that specifies how a competence is expected to be addressed within a particular instructional unit.

In OntoKSD, CompetenceActivation associates exactly one Competence through mediatesCompetence with exactly one InstructionalUnit through mediatesInstructionalUnit. It makes the association independently identifiable so that its pedagogical qualifications can be recorded without modifying the reusable competence definition.

An activation may be qualified through four complementary dimensions:

• ActivationFunction identifies the pedagogical purpose of the association, such as addressing the competence as an instructional target or presupposing it as a prerequisite.

• ActivationRole identifies the competence's contribution within the instructional unit, such as central, supporting, or transversal.

• ActivationMode identifies the expected form of engagement, such as constructive, analytical, interpretative, or justificatory.

• ActivationConstraint specifies an applicable condition, with its content recorded through hasActivationConstraintDescription and its status classified through hasActivationConstraintType.

These dimensions are associated through hasActivationFunction, hasActivationRole, hasActivationMode, and hasActivationConstraint. Multiple qualifications may coexist where their meanings and the instructional specification support a coherent interpretation. Shared classificatory values do not identify distinct activation associations as the same entity.

The same Competence may participate in separate activations across instructional units, allowing its pedagogical purpose, contribution, engagement modes, and conditions to vary while preserving its definition. One instructional unit may likewise address several competences through separate activation instances.

CompetenceActivation belongs to the instructional specification layer. The term activation denotes planned pedagogical mobilization rather than an event in which a learner actually exercises a competence. Its existence does not establish participation, completed performance, satisfaction of a condition, or learner attainment.

The association is distinct from CourseCompetenceSpecification, which records curricular inclusion and expectations at the course level. It is also distinct from the association between a Competence and its reference Task. Task describes the performance addressed by the competence specification, whereas InstructionalUnit describes the planned pedagogical work through which that competence is addressed.

An activation does not replace the complete instructional description. Actions, deliverables, and conditions of a LearningTask must remain recordable independently of competence associations, including requirements for which no activation has been asserted.

Changes in instructional qualifications must remain consistent with the associated competence. An activation does not automatically establish competence composition or specialization, nor does it authorize a silent change to required knowledge, capabilities, dispositions, or skill levels.

CompetenceActivation supports the traceability of instructional intentions while maintaining the separation between competence specifications, curricular expectations, instructional planning, and learner-specific evidence and judgments.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceConstituent
ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              owl:disjointWith ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence ,
                                               ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent ;
                              dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Cited as Competency in Miranda, S. et al. (2018). An Ontology-Based Model for Competence Management. Journal of Intelligent Manufacturing."@en ;
                              rdfs:comment """An element of a competence specification that describes part of the knowledge, capability, dispositional orientation, or knowledge–performance pairing required for expected competent performance.

In OntoKSD, CompetenceConstituent provides a common superclass for Knowledge, Skill, Disposition, and KnowledgeSkill. These subclasses share a structural role in competence specification while retaining distinct meanings:

• Knowledge identifies the conceptual or content-oriented element involved in expected performance.

• Skill identifies an explicitly specified capability or ability relevant to expected performance.

• Disposition identifies an inclination, attitude, belief, value, or other orientation expected to shape competent action. Attitude is represented as a specialization of Disposition.

• KnowledgeSkill represents a structured pairing between a Knowledge element and a required SkillLevel. It specifies the performance expected in relation to that knowledge without identifying the pairing with either Knowledge or Skill, or treating SkillLevel as an individuated skill.

This abstraction supports the KS, KSA, KSD, and KSD-T specification patterns. The adopted SpecificationPattern determines which constituent types are required; CompetenceConstituent does not itself require every competence to include all constituent types.

The class provides three principal modeling functions: a common target for hasCompetenceConstituent and its specialized relations; uniform retrieval of constituents across different specification patterns; and a common reference for constituent-level evidential records through EvidencedCompetenceConstituent.

A CompetenceConstituent belongs to the specification layer. It does not represent a learner's actual knowledge, capability, disposition, or demonstrated attainment. Evidence and proficiency interpretations concerning an individual's demonstration of a constituent are represented separately through EvidencedCompetenceConstituent and its associated relations.

CompetenceConstituent is a structural abstraction for organizing specification elements, rather than an assertion that its subclasses share the same foundational ontological nature. Its specialization is not intended to be exhaustively restricted to Knowledge, Skill, and Disposition, allowing KnowledgeSkill and other explicitly justified constituent types to be accommodated.

Task is not classified as a CompetenceConstituent. It is associated directly with Competence to express the reference task addressed by the specification, including the explicit task required by the KSD-T pattern. Likewise, the constitutive relation used here expresses membership in a semantic specification and does not, by itself, assert physical or object-level parthood.""" ;
                              rdfs:label "CompetenceConstituent"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceScope
ontoksd:CompetenceScope rdf:type owl:Class ;
                        rdfs:comment """CompetenceScope
A classificatory category used to indicate the scope or applicability of a Competence within educational and professional contexts. It distinguishes whether a competence is specific to the Computing domain, foundational for broader learning and development, professional in orientation, or transversal across domains and contexts.

CompetenceScope is intended to support the semantic classification of competencies according to their breadth of applicability and pedagogical or professional orientation. This classification helps differentiate competencies that are domain-specific from those that are broadly transferable or oriented toward professional practice.

Instances of CompetenceScope should be used as controlled values associated with Competence through a property such as hasCompetenceScope.

Instance: computingSpecific

computingSpecific
A scope classification indicating that a competence is specific to the Computing domain. Competencies with this scope are directly related to computing concepts, methods, techniques, practices, or problem-solving activities, and are not primarily intended as general-purpose or cross-domain competencies.

Examples include competencies related to programming, algorithms, data structures, software design, computer systems, artificial intelligence, or other core areas of Computing.

Instance: foundational

foundational
A scope classification indicating that a competence provides essential grounding for learning, reasoning, or performance and serves as a basis for the development of other competencies. Foundational competencies are not necessarily exclusive to Computing, but they are often required to support more specific or advanced forms of competence.

Examples may include logical reasoning, mathematical thinking, abstraction, problem interpretation, or other competencies that function as conceptual or cognitive foundations for further learning and practice.

Instance: professional

professional
A scope classification indicating that a competence is oriented toward professional performance, workplace practice, or the exercise of responsibilities in occupational settings. Competencies with this scope are associated with professional conduct, collaboration, project execution, communication, ethics, or other capabilities relevant to professional activity.

This scope may include competencies that are valued in Computing professions but are not limited to domain-specific technical knowledge alone.

Instance: transversal

transversal
A scope classification indicating that a competence is transferable across multiple domains, disciplines, or contexts. Competencies with this scope are not restricted to a single knowledge area or professional field and may be mobilized in varied educational, social, or occupational situations.

Examples include communication, teamwork, autonomy, critical thinking, adaptability, and other broadly applicable competencies that can support performance across different contexts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompositeCompetence
ontoksd:CompositeCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:Competence
                                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasComponentCompetence ;
                                                                         owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Competence
                                                                       ]
                                                                     ) ;
                                                  rdf:type owl:Class
                                                ] ,
                                                [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:Competence
                                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasStructureType ;
                                                                         owl:hasValue ontoksd:Composite
                                                                       ]
                                                                     ) ;
                                                  rdf:type owl:Class
                                                ] ;
                            rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Competence ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasStructureType ;
                                              owl:hasValue ontoksd:Composite
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasComponentCompetence ;
                                              owl:minQualifiedCardinality "2"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                            ] ;
                            rdfs:comment """CompositeCompetence

A competence specification whose expected performance requires the integration of two or more component competence specifications.

In OntoKSD, CompositeCompetence is a subclass of Competence associated with the StructureType value Composite. Its component competences are identified through hasComponentCompetence. Each component may itself be atomic or composite, supporting hierarchical decomposition.

Composition expresses more than membership in a collection. The composite specification must describe how its components contribute to an integrated performance. Sharing subject matter, appearing in the same curriculum, or being addressed by the same instructional activity does not, by itself, establish competence composition.

The intended integration should be explicitly documented. It may concern coordination, sequencing, mutual constraints, or the production of a coherent outcome that depends on the combined contribution of the components.

Component competences remain independently identifiable and may be reused in different composite specifications. Composition does not, by itself, impose exclusive ownership or transfer all constituent assertions from the components to the composite.

The hasComponent relation identifies direct components. Indirect components may be retrieved by traversing successive composition relations. A competence must not be directly or indirectly composed of itself.

CompositeCompetence is disjoint with AtomicCompetence. Evidence concerning individual components does not automatically establish evidence of their integration or attainment of the composite competence.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Context
ontoksd:Context rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Situation> ;
                rdfs:comment """A situational configuration of circumstances relevant to the applicability, demonstration, observation, or interpretation of a competence or its constituents.

In OntoKSD, Context is a subclass of gufo:Situation. It represents a configuration in which relevant entities stand in particular relations, rather than an activity, an event, a physical location, or an undifferentiated contextual label.

A Context may describe environmental, organizational, social, temporal, task-related, or domain-specific circumstances. Its description should identify which circumstances are relevant and how they frame the competence specification, evidence, or interpretation concerned. A location or resource may contribute to that configuration without being identical to the context itself.

Competence specifications, constituents, evidence, and competence-related evidential records may be associated with Context through hasContext, whose inverse is providesContextFor. The meaning of the association depends on the entity contextualized: a specification may identify intended applicability, whereas an evidential record may identify circumstances relevant to interpreting a particular demonstration.

Intended circumstances and recorded circumstances must remain distinguishable. A context associated with expected performance does not establish that those conditions occurred, and a context recorded for one demonstration does not automatically delimit every possible application of the reusable competence.

Context is distinct from Task, which specifies the reference performance addressed by a competence, and from ActivationConstraint, which records a condition applicable to a particular instructional association. A constraint may describe part of a contextual configuration without representing that configuration as a whole.

Context is not restricted to education. AcquisitionContext specializes it for teaching, learning, training, and assessment. Examples include a configuration of responsibilities, resources, and constraints in a workplace, or the collaboration and tool conditions relevant to solving a problem.

Where interpretation depends on a time interval or other boundaries, these should be explicitly documented. Relevant changes in circumstances should be represented without silently altering the context used to interpret existing records.

Associating an entity with Context supports contextualized interpretation. It does not, by itself, establish competence attainment, satisfaction of conditions, causal explanations of performance, or transfer of a demonstrated capability to another context.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Course
ontoksd:Course rdf:type owl:Class ;
               owl:disjointWith ontoksd:EducationalResource ,
                                ontoksd:InstructionalUnit .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CourseCompetenceSpecification
ontoksd:CourseCompetenceSpecification rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                      rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ,
                                                      [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesCourse ;
                                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:Course
                                                                             ]
                                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesCourseCompetence ;
                                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                                                             ]
                                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:requiresMinimumProficiencyLevel ;
                                                                               owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                                                             ]
                                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:isMandatory ;
                                                                               owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                                               owl:onDataRange xsd:boolean
                                                                             ]
                                                                           ) ;
                                                        rdf:type owl:Class
                                                      ] ;
                                      rdfs:comment """A qualified curricular association that records the inclusion of a competence specification within a course and the expectations attached to that inclusion.

In OntoKSD, CourseCompetenceSpecification associates exactly one Course through mediatesCourse with exactly one Competence through mediatesCourseCompetence. It makes the course-specific expectation independently identifiable while preserving the reusable definition of the competence.

Each specification records mandatory status through isMandatory. A true value expresses a curricular commitment to address the competence within the course. A false value records its inclusion without that mandatory commitment. Neither value establishes an admission prerequisite, a course-passing criterion, or learner attainment.

A CourseCompetenceSpecification may identify at most one minimum expected proficiency level through requiresMinimumProficiencyLevel. This expresses the minimum degree of performance that the course is intended to address for the associated competence. It is not a SkillLevel qualifying a knowledge–performance pairing, a numeric assessment score, or a proficiency judgment about an individual learner.

The applicable proficiency scheme and the ordering or descriptors that give meaning to the minimum level should be identifiable. The same ProficiencyLevel category may be referenced both in curricular expectations and in learner-specific judgments; these uses remain distinct because of the associations in which the category participates.

The same Competence may be included in different courses through separate CourseCompetenceSpecification instances. Mandatory status and expected proficiency may vary between these associations without silently changing the competence definition. Where curricular changes alter the competence specification itself, that change must be identified separately.

CourseCompetenceSpecification belongs to the curricular expectation layer. It is distinct from CompetenceActivation, which qualifies how a competence is addressed within a particular InstructionalUnit. Declaring curricular inclusion does not automatically establish an instructional association, sufficient instructional coverage, or fulfillment of the curricular expectation. These correspondences require explicitly recorded information and separate examination.

The inclusion of several competences within a course does not, by itself, establish composition or specialization between them. Likewise, this association does not represent delivered instruction, learner participation, collected evidence, or an evaluative judgment.

Representing the association explicitly keeps its qualifications attached to the course–competence relationship rather than treating them as intrinsic properties of either participant. It supports curricular traceability and comparison while preserving the distinction between expected performance, curricular commitments, instructional planning, and learner-specific records.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Disposition
ontoksd:Disposition rdf:type owl:Class ;
                    rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ;
                    rdfs:comment """A competence constituent that specifies an orientation expected to influence whether and how knowledge and skills are mobilized in competent performance.

In OntoKSD, Disposition represents the dispositional requirements of a competence specification, including inclination-oriented, attitudinal, value-based, belief-based, and motivational aspects relevant to purposeful action. It describes an expected orientation rather than asserting that a particular individual possesses a psychological characteristic or has demonstrated a pattern of behavior.

This interpretation is grounded in CC2020, which describes dispositions as habitual inclinations influencing whether and how individuals use their skills, and in CS2023, which characterizes professional dispositions in terms of malleable values, beliefs, and attitudes associated with consistent behaviors desirable in professional practice.

Disposition is distinct from Skill. Skill specifies a capability to perform an activity, whereas Disposition specifies an orientation toward exercising that capability. For example, the capability to collaborate concerns Skill, while the willingness to engage constructively with others concerns Disposition. Classification depends on the meaning of the specified element rather than on its label alone.

Attitude is modeled as a specialization of Disposition. Within OntoKSD, attitudinal requirements are dispositional requirements distinguished by their evaluative orientation toward an object, practice, behavior, or situation relevant to competent performance. Other dispositions, such as persistence or meticulousness, need not be classified as Attitude unless their definitions support that interpretation.

A Disposition may be associated with a Competence through hasDisposition and classified through hasDispositionCategory. The categories used for description need not constitute mutually exclusive or exhaustive subclasses.

Disposition belongs to the specification layer. Learner-specific evidence concerning its manifestation is represented separately through EvidencedCompetenceConstituent and associated assessment and evidence relations. A single observed action does not, by itself, establish a stable disposition, and evidence in one context does not automatically establish its manifestation in other contexts.

The class supports the dispositional dimension of the CC2020 and CS2023 specification patterns while accommodating attitudinal requirements from KSA specifications through Attitude. Its name and educational meaning do not, by themselves, establish equivalence with a foundational ontology category such as gufo:Disposition."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#DispositionCategory
ontoksd:DispositionCategory rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:comment """DispositionCategory
A classificatory category used to indicate the predominant dispositional nature of a Disposition element within a competency specification. In OntoKSD, this class supports the semantic classification of dispositions according to whether they are primarily attitudinal, belief-based, or value-based.

This classification is intended to provide a lightweight and semantically coherent mechanism for organizing dispositional elements without overcomplicating the ontology. It helps distinguish different kinds of dispositional orientation while preserving the broader unifying role of the class Disposition.

Instances of DispositionCategory should be used as controlled values associated with Disposition through a property such as hasDispositionCategory.""" ,
                                         """Instance: attitudeCategory

attitudeCategory
A disposition category indicating that a disposition is predominantly attitudinal in nature. Dispositions classified under this category primarily express evaluative or affective orientations toward objects, situations, practices, people, or forms of action.

Examples may include openness to collaboration, willingness to engage, persistence toward challenges, or responsibility toward task completion when these are modeled primarily as attitudinal tendencies.

Instance: beliefCategory

beliefCategory
A disposition category indicating that a disposition is predominantly belief-based in nature. Dispositions classified under this category primarily express convictions, assumptions, or internalized understandings that shape how a person interprets situations and approaches action.

Examples may include beliefs about the value of systematic reasoning, the importance of ethical responsibility, or the relevance of collaboration in professional practice.

Instance: valueCategory

valueCategory
A disposition category indicating that a disposition is predominantly value-based in nature. Dispositions classified under this category primarily express normative commitments, principles, or value orientations that guide judgment and behavior.

Examples may include valuing inclusiveness, valuing professional integrity, valuing accuracy, or valuing social responsibility in the design and use of computing artefacts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Document
ontoksd:Document rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:comment """A documentary artefact that records, contains, organizes, or supports evidence related to one or more evidenced competencies.

In OntoKSD, Document represents a material or digital record that may serve as documentary support for evidence. Examples may include certificates, reports, transcripts, portfolios, declarations, or other records that document competence-related achievements or performances.

A Document may aggregate one or more instances of Evidence and may serve as a supporting artefact for the identification of evidenced competencies or competence constituents.

Document is intentionally modeled as a general documentary class, without committing to finer distinctions such as validation records, certificates, or internal document sections, unless such distinctions are needed elsewhere in the ontology.""" ;
                 rdfs:label "Document"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EducationalResource
ontoksd:EducationalResource rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            owl:disjointWith ontoksd:InstructionalUnit ;
                            rdfs:comment """EducationalResource
A pedagogical artefact used to support, mediate, document, or facilitate teaching, learning, or assessment processes within an instructional context.

In OntoKSD, EducationalResource is modeled as an entity distinct from InstructionalUnit. It does not represent an instructional action, plan, or event in itself, but rather an artefact that may be used within instructional contexts. This decision avoids conflating instructional units, which are the primary loci of competence activation, with the informational or material artefacts that support them.

Examples of educational resources may include textbooks, articles, slide decks, videos, worksheets, datasets, simulators, source code, rubrics, manuals, or other pedagogically relevant materials.

An EducationalResource may therefore be associated with instructional units through relations such as usesResource, indicating that the resource is employed within a learning activity, learning task, assessment, or other instructional unit.

At the current scope of OntoKSD, EducationalResource is intentionally modeled in a lightweight manner. Its minimal descriptive structure includes properties such as title and description, while its semantic classification may be expressed through controlled concepts, such as SKOS concepts associated with resource-type schemes.

Ontological rationale:
EducationalResource is modeled as a pedagogical artefact rather than as an instructional unit in order to preserve the distinction between pedagogical action and the artefacts used to support it. Instructional units remain the contexts in which competencies are activated, while educational resources function as supporting artefacts within those contexts.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Evidence
ontoksd:Evidence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 owl:disjointWith ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence ,
                                  ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent ;
                 rdfs:comment """A record, artefact, or observable element that supports the identification of a competence or competence constituent as evidenced in a given context.

In OntoKSD, Evidence represents any element used to indicate that a competence or one of its constituent elements has been demonstrated, manifested, or otherwise made observable. It functions as a bridge between competence specifications and their concrete evidencing in educational, professional, or other relevant situations.

An Evidence may be associated with a specific EvidenceType, may be linked to a supporting Document, and may support one or more instances of EvidencedCompetence or EvidencedCompetenceConstituent.

Evidence is intentionally modeled in a lightweight manner. Its role is not to represent the full validation, interpretation, or certification process of performance, but simply to denote an element that supports the recognition that a competence has been evidenced.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidenceType
ontoksd:EvidenceType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:comment """A classificatory category that identifies the form or evidential nature of an Evidence instance.

In OntoKSD, EvidenceType provides reusable controlled values associated with Evidence through hasEvidenceType. Examples include a written response, presentation recording, portfolio artefact, documented performance observation, simulation result, or technical report.

Evidence classification concerns the collected artefact or documented observation, rather than the assessment specification or the judgment supported by that evidence. An examination design is an Assessment; a learner's completed response or a record produced during its execution may be Evidence.

An Evidence instance may have multiple compatible classifications. Where the vocabulary includes different dimensions, such as artefact form, reporting source, or observation method, their meanings should remain explicit. Values such as self-reported, peer-reported, or assessor-observed describe provenance or collection perspectives rather than artefact forms.

EvidenceType is distinct from the assessment-related concepts associated through hasAssessmentTypeConcept. Classification as peer-reported or self-reported does not identify the actual reporting agent, establish an assessment occurrence, or replace participant-specific traceability.

Type values do not determine whether evidence is authentic, valid, sufficient, reliable, or supportive of a particular judgment. Those interpretations require separately identifiable criteria, contextual information, and supporting records.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidencedCompetence
ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:supportedByEvidence ;
                                              owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:Evidence
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:derivedFromParticipation ;
                                              owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:AssessmentParticipation
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:isEvidencedCompetenceOf ;
                                              owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:Profile
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:refersToCompetence ;
                                              owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                            ] ,
                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasProficiencyLevel ;
                                              owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                              owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                            ] ;
                            owl:disjointWith ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent ;
                            rdfs:comment """A contextualized, learner-specific record of an evidence-based judgment concerning performance in relation to a defined Competence.

In OntoKSD, EvidencedCompetence distinguishes the reusable specification of expected performance from its interpretation for an individual learner on a particular assessment occasion. It is neither the competence specification nor the evidence itself.

Each record refers to exactly one Competence through refersToCompetence, is grounded in exactly one AssessmentParticipation through derivedFromParticipation, and is supported by one or more Evidence instances through supportedByEvidence. The participation identifies the learner and assessment occurrence. The record is associated with a Profile through isEvidencedCompetenceOf, whose owner must agree with the learner identified by the participation.

An EvidencedCompetence may have a ProficiencyLevel expressing the recorded interpretation of demonstrated performance. The applicable proficiency scheme, relevant criteria, and supporting evidence should remain identifiable. A proficiency category is distinct from a numeric assessment result, and the record does not necessarily establish complete mastery, successful completion, or transfer to other contexts.

Separate records preserve judgments concerning different assessment occasions, even when the learner and competence are unchanged. Relevant dates distinguish the occasion of performance from the recording or validation of its interpretation. A sequence of records does not, by itself, establish progression.

Finer-grained records may be associated through hasEvidencedConstituent. Their relationship to the whole-competence judgment must be explicit and consistent with the learner, occasion, and competence concerned. Constituent-level results do not automatically aggregate into a whole-competence judgment. For a CompositeCompetence, evidence of its components does not, by itself, establish their integrated performance.

Evidence may be shared across learners, but its role in supporting each individual judgment must remain explicit. Associating evidence with an assessment occurrence or profile does not automatically make it supporting evidence for every related record.

EvidencedCompetence records an assessment interpretation under identifiable conditions. It does not independently certify the validity of the judgment or assert a context-independent capability possessed by the learner."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidencedCompetenceConstituent
ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceConstituent rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                       rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:supportedByEvidence ;
                                                         owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:Evidence
                                                       ] ,
                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:derivedFromParticipation ;
                                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:AssessmentParticipation
                                                       ] ,
                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:refersToCompetenceConstituent ;
                                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent
                                                       ] ,
                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasProficiencyLevel ;
                                                         owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                                       ] ,
                                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:isEvidencedCompetenceConstituentOf ;
                                                         owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence
                                                       ] ;
                                       rdfs:comment """A contextualized, learner-specific record of an evidence-based judgment concerning performance in relation to a defined CompetenceConstituent.

In OntoKSD, EvidencedCompetenceConstituent distinguishes a reusable constituent specification from its interpretation for an individual learner on a particular assessment occasion. The referenced constituent may be Knowledge, Skill, Disposition, including Attitude, or a KnowledgeSkill pairing specifying knowledge-related performance at a required skill level.

Each record refers to exactly one constituent through refersToCompetenceConstituent, is grounded in exactly one AssessmentParticipation through derivedFromParticipation, and is supported by one or more Evidence instances through supportedByEvidence. The participation identifies the learner and assessment occurrence.

A record may have a ProficiencyLevel expressing the interpretation of performance concerning that constituent. The applicable proficiency scheme, relevant criteria, and supporting evidence should remain identifiable. The attributed proficiency level is distinct from the SkillLevel required by a KnowledgeSkill specification and from a numeric assessment result.

An EvidencedCompetenceConstituent may be linked to an EvidencedCompetence through isEvidencedCompetenceConstituentOf. This linkage identifies its relationship to a whole-competence judgment; it does not imply automatic aggregation. Where linked, the learner, assessment occasion, and referenced constituent must be consistent with the associated competence record. A constituent-level judgment does not, by itself, establish a judgment about the competence as a whole.

For a Disposition or Attitude constituent, the record concerns evidence interpreted under the stated assessment conditions. A single observed action does not necessarily establish a stable personal orientation or its manifestation across other contexts.

Separate records preserve interpretations from different occasions. Shared evidence does not automatically support identical judgments for different learners or constituents, and a sequence of records does not, by itself, establish progression.

The class supports detailed assessment, profiling, and feedback while preserving the distinction between constituent specifications, supporting evidence, and individual judgments. It does not independently establish judgment validity, complete mastery, or attainment of an encompassing competence."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ExternalCompetence
ontoksd:ExternalCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:comment """An identifiable reference to a competence specification defined in an external curricular framework, standard, normative document, or institutional guideline.

In OntoKSD, ExternalCompetence preserves the identity and meaning of a source-defined competence without automatically imposing the constituent structure or specification patterns adopted for internal Competence instances.

An external reference should identify its source, identifier or code where available, authoritative statement, relevant edition or version, and applicable curricular scope. Its representation should distinguish the source wording from local interpretations or adaptations.

ExternalCompetence may be represented independently of any alignment. An asserted correspondence with an internal Competence is documented through ExternalCompetenceAlignment, including its principal type, justification, and scope restrictions. Merely citing an external competence does not establish such correspondence.

The class is distinct from an internal specification created or adapted for use within OntoKSD. Where an internal specification operationalizes an external expectation, their relationship should be explicitly documented rather than inferred from similar labels or shared subject matter.

ExternalCompetence does not automatically encompass every kind of curricular statement. Items identified by their source as skills, knowledge topics, learning objectives, or other categories require an explicit representation decision rather than automatic classification as external competences.

The class supports reference management, curricular comparison, and traceable alignment. Its use does not, by itself, establish semantic equivalence, complete coverage, regulatory compliance, or learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ExternalCompetenceAlignment
ontoksd:ExternalCompetenceAlignment rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                    rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ,
                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasAlignmentType ;
                                                      owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                      owl:onClass ontoksd:AlignmentType
                                                    ] ,
                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesExternalCompetence ;
                                                      owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                      owl:onClass ontoksd:ExternalCompetence
                                                    ] ,
                                                    [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                      owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesInternalCompetence ;
                                                      owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                                      owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                                    ] ;
                                    rdfs:comment """A qualified record of an asserted correspondence between an internal competence specification and an external competence reference.

In OntoKSD, ExternalCompetenceAlignment associates exactly one Competence through mediatesInternalCompetence with exactly one ExternalCompetence through mediatesExternalCompetence. Each record identifies exactly one principal AlignmentType through hasAlignmentType.

The alignment interpretation is documented through hasAlignmentJustification. Its explanation should identify the basis of comparison, the corresponding aspects, and relevant differences or scope restrictions. Supporting sources and the versions of the compared specifications should remain identifiable.

Directional classifications are interpreted from the internal specification toward the external reference. A broader alignment asserts that the internal scope encompasses the external scope; a narrower alignment asserts that the internal scope addresses a restricted portion of the external scope. Partial correspondences must make the limits of coverage explicit.

The same internal competence may have separate alignments with multiple external references, and an external reference may be related to multiple internal competences. Each record preserves the qualifications of its particular correspondence.

ExternalCompetenceAlignment is distinct from merely identifying or citing an external statement. Its existence expresses an alignment assertion, not an automatic consequence of shared terminology, subject matter, or inclusion in the same curriculum.

The record does not automatically establish identity, logical equivalence, specialization, composition, prerequisite relationships, learner attainment, or regulatory compliance. Such conclusions require independently justified representations or evaluation.

The class supports traceable comparison while preserving the definitions and identities of the internal and external specifications. Revisions to their meanings or to the alignment interpretation should be explicitly documented rather than silently changing the basis of existing correspondences."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#InstructionalUnit
ontoksd:InstructionalUnit rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:disjointWith ontoksd:Task ;
                          rdfs:comment """A bounded pedagogical specification that organizes expected learner engagement, practice, or assessment within an educational context.

In OntoKSD, InstructionalUnit provides a general class for LearningActivity, LearningTask, and Assessment. It represents planned instruction rather than an occurrence of its execution, a learner's participation, or evidence that an expected performance has taken place.

An InstructionalUnit specifies a pedagogically meaningful unit of work or engagement. Its description may identify objectives, expected actions, deliverables, resources, and applicable conditions. These requirements may be recorded independently of competence associations: a unit can be represented before any CompetenceActivation has been asserted, and retrieving its associated competences does not necessarily recover its complete instructional description.

Competence-related expectations are represented through CompetenceActivation. Each activation associates one Competence with one InstructionalUnit and may qualify its pedagogical function, contribution, mode of engagement, and applicable conditions. An instructional unit may have multiple such associations, allowing different competences to be addressed in different ways without modifying their reusable definitions.

An InstructionalUnit may be associated with an AcquisitionContext through hasAcquisitionContext and with supporting EducationalResource instances through usesResource. The contextual association describes the conditions for which the instruction is specified; it does not establish that an execution has occurred. When contextual variation changes relevant requirements or competence qualifications, that variation should be explicitly represented rather than silently incorporated into an existing specification.

InstructionalUnit is distinct from Course, which provides curricular organization, and from EducationalResource, which provides an artefact used to support instruction. It is also distinct from Task, which expresses the reference performance addressed by a competence specification. LearningTask specializes InstructionalUnit through LearningActivity and operationalizes learner work at the instructional level without being identified with the competence-level Task.

A Course may organize instructional units through hasInstructionalUnit. This curricular association does not, by itself, establish that the units provide sufficient coverage of the course's competence expectations.

InstructionalUnit may be identified and documented through an identifier, title, instructional statement, and description. These descriptive elements complement its explicit competence, context, and resource associations.

The class supports the separation of curricular expectations, instructional design, execution, and evidence. Its existence and its competence associations express educational intentions rather than learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KSACompetence
ontoksd:KSACompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:Competence
                                                                 [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                   owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSpecificationPattern ;
                                                                   owl:hasValue ontoksd:KSASpecificationPattern
                                                                 ]
                                                               ) ;
                                            rdf:type owl:Class
                                          ] ;
                      rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Competence ,
                                      [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasAttitude ;
                                                               owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Attitude
                                                             ]
                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasKnowledge ;
                                                               owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Knowledge
                                                             ]
                                                             [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSkill ;
                                                               owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Skill
                                                             ]
                                                           ) ;
                                        rdf:type owl:Class
                                      ] ;
                      rdfs:comment """KSACompetence

A competence specification that adopts the Knowledge–Skill–Attitude (KSA) specification pattern.

In OntoKSD, KSACompetence is defined as a Competence associated with KSASpecificationPattern through hasSpecificationPattern. Its minimum constituent structure includes at least one Knowledge element through hasKnowledge, at least one Skill element through hasSkill, and at least one Attitude element through hasAttitude.

Knowledge identifies the content involved in the expected performance, Skill identifies the expected capability, and Attitude identifies an attitudinal orientation expected to guide that performance.

Because Attitude is a subclass of Disposition, each attitude constituent is also a disposition constituent. However, a generic Disposition element does not, by itself, satisfy the requirement for an Attitude element in the KSA pattern.

An explicitly represented Task is not required by this pattern. KSACompetence represents the expected specification of knowledge, skills, and attitudes, rather than evidence that an individual possesses or has demonstrated them.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KSCompetence
ontoksd:KSCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     owl:equivalentClass [ owl:intersectionOf ( ontoksd:Competence
                                                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSpecificationPattern ;
                                                                  owl:hasValue ontoksd:KSSpecificationPattern
                                                                ]
                                                              ) ;
                                           rdf:type owl:Class
                                         ] ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Competence ,
                                     [ owl:intersectionOf ( [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasKnowledge ;
                                                              owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Knowledge
                                                            ]
                                                            [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                                              owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSkill ;
                                                              owl:someValuesFrom ontoksd:Skill
                                                            ]
                                                          ) ;
                                       rdf:type owl:Class
                                     ] ;
                     rdfs:comment """KSCompetence

A competence specification that adopts the Knowledge–Skill (KS) specification pattern.

In OntoKSD, KSCompetence is defined as a Competence associated with KSSpecificationPattern through hasSpecificationPattern. Its minimum constituent structure includes at least one Knowledge element through hasKnowledge and at least one Skill element through hasSkill.

Knowledge identifies the content involved in the expected performance, while Skill identifies what the learner or practitioner is expected to be able to do. A Skill constituent is distinct from a SkillLevel, which may qualify the expected performance but does not itself represent an individuated skill.

The KS pattern does not require an attitude or disposition constituent, nor an explicitly represented Task. These minimum requirements do not prohibit additional specification elements.

KSCompetence represents expected performance, rather than a learner's demonstrated knowledge, skills, or attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Knowledge
ontoksd:Knowledge rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ;
                  rdfs:comment """A competence constituent that specifies the knowledge content required for expected competent performance in a given domain.

In OntoKSD, Knowledge represents facts, concepts, principles, theories, and procedures referenced in a competence specification. It identifies what the expected performance draws upon, rather than asserting what a particular individual knows or has demonstrated.

Knowledge may encompass declarative content, such as facts and conceptual relationships, and procedural content, such as methods, techniques, and procedures. Procedural knowledge describes the content of a procedure; Skill specifies the capability to perform or mobilize it. These elements may be related without being treated as equivalent.

A Knowledge element may be associated directly with a Competence through hasKnowledge or participate in a KnowledgeSkill pairing through hasKnowledgeComponent. In the latter case, the required SkillLevel qualifies the performance expected in relation to that knowledge within the pairing, rather than assigning an intrinsic performance level to the Knowledge element itself.

Knowledge elements may be reused across competence specifications and paired with different required performance levels. They may also be classified through hasKnowledgeType and linked to curricular concepts through isCurricularlyRepresentedBy, preserving the distinction between knowledge content and its organization in a curricular framework.

Knowledge belongs to the specification layer. Learner-specific evidence concerning its demonstration is represented separately through EvidencedCompetenceConstituent and associated evidence and assessment relations. A reference to a Knowledge element therefore does not, by itself, establish that an individual has acquired, understood, or successfully applied that knowledge."""@en ;
                  rdfs:label "Knowledge"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KnowledgeSkill
ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ,
                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasKnowledgeComponent ;
                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                         owl:onClass ontoksd:Knowledge
                                       ] ,
                                       [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                         owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSkillLevel ;
                                         owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                         owl:onClass <http://purl.org/edu/bloom#SkillLevel>
                                       ] ;
                       rdfs:comment """A competence constituent that specifies a required level of performance in relation to a particular Knowledge element.

In OntoKSD, KnowledgeSkill explicitly represents a knowledge-specific performance requirement. It associates exactly one Knowledge element, through hasKnowledgeComponent, with exactly one required SkillLevel, through hasRequiredSkillLevel. A Competence incorporates this requirement through hasKnowledgeSkill.

The principal justification for KnowledgeSkill is the preservation of the correspondence between knowledge and expected performance. Listing several knowledge elements and several skill levels independently within a competence would not establish which level applies to which knowledge element. KnowledgeSkill makes each association explicit, preventing the loss of meaning that would result from representing these requirements as unrelated lists.

For example, a competence may require Applying in relation to finite automata and Analyzing in relation to algorithmic complexity. Two KnowledgeSkill instances preserve these distinct requirements. Separate links from the competence to both knowledge elements and both levels would leave their intended correspondence unspecified.

KnowledgeSkill also allows the same Knowledge element to participate in different performance requirements without assigning a single intrinsic level to that knowledge. One competence may require Understanding of a knowledge element, while another requires Applying or Creating in relation to the same element. The required level belongs to the pairing used in the specification, rather than to the Knowledge element in isolation.

KnowledgeSkill is distinct from both Knowledge and Skill. Knowledge identifies the content involved; Skill specifies an explicitly identified capability; KnowledgeSkill specifies the level of performance required in relation to particular knowledge content. A SkillLevel qualifies expected performance and is not itself an individuated Skill. The name KnowledgeSkill therefore denotes the knowledge–performance pairing used in the specification, rather than an intersection of the Knowledge and Skill classes.

This construct supports the representation of the KSD and KSD-T patterns adopted in OntoKSD for CC2020SpecificationPattern and CS2023SpecificationPattern. It preserves knowledge-specific performance requirements while allowing other patterns to represent Knowledge and Skill as separate constituents.

As an explicitly represented specification element, KnowledgeSkill can be referenced uniformly through CompetenceConstituent, retrieved as part of a competence's structure, and addressed by EvidencedCompetenceConstituent. It also provides a reference point for documenting the rationale for a required level, including justificatory action verbs through hasBloomLevelJustificationVerb. Such supporting information explains the specification decision without replacing its constitutive knowledge and level associations.

KnowledgeSkill is modeled as a specification element, not as a gufo:Relator. Representing an association explicitly does not, by itself, establish the existential dependence and mediation commitments required by that foundational category. Its inclusion under CompetenceConstituent reflects its structural function within a competence specification.

The knowledge element and required level determine the semantic content of the pairing. The cardinality restrictions ensure one value for each association but do not, by themselves, establish that two instances with the same values are identical. Any policy for identifying, reusing, or distinguishing such instances must be specified separately.

KnowledgeSkill belongs to the specification layer. It expresses required performance in relation to knowledge, rather than asserting that a learner has attained that level. Learner-specific demonstrations, supporting evidence, and proficiency interpretations are represented separately through the evidential layer.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KnowledgeType
ontoksd:KnowledgeType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:comment """KnowledgeType
A classificatory category used to indicate the type or orientation of a Knowledge element within a competency specification. In OntoKSD, this class is used to distinguish different kinds of knowledge that may contribute to the constitution of a competence, particularly with respect to their role in computing education and practice.

This classification is aligned with the competency-oriented perspective of CC2020, which distinguishes knowledge that is specifically related to Computing from knowledge that is foundational, professional, or associated with an application domain. Accordingly, KnowledgeType supports the semantic organization of knowledge elements according to their educational and professional orientation.

Instances of KnowledgeType should be used as controlled values associated with Knowledge through a property such as hasKnowledgeType.

Suggested instances
computingSpecific

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element is specific to the Computing domain. It includes concepts, principles, methods, models, techniques, and bodies of knowledge that are characteristically associated with Computing as a discipline or profession.

Examples include knowledge related to algorithms, data structures, programming, software design, computer systems, networks, databases, artificial intelligence, and other core areas of Computing.

foundational

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element provides foundational support for learning, reasoning, or practice. Foundational knowledge is not exclusive to Computing, but it is essential for understanding, applying, or advancing computing-related competencies.

Examples may include mathematical reasoning, logic, communication foundations, scientific literacy, and other forms of knowledge that support learning and performance in computing.

professional

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element is associated with professional practice, workplace conduct, or the organizational and social dimensions of computing work. This includes knowledge that supports responsible, effective, and context-aware participation in professional environments.

Examples may include knowledge related to teamwork, communication practices, ethics, workplace norms, project practices, accountability, and professional expectations.

applicationDomain

A knowledge type indicating that a knowledge element belongs to the domain in which computing is applied. This type captures knowledge of the social, organizational, commercial, scientific, or sector-specific context that informs the design, deployment, or use of computing artefacts and solutions.

Examples may include knowledge related to business, health, education, transportation, public administration, finance, or other domains in which computing competencies are exercised.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Learner
ontoksd:Learner rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Person ,
                                [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasProfile ;
                                  owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                  owl:onClass ontoksd:Profile
                                ] .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningActivity
ontoksd:LearningActivity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:InstructionalUnit ;
                         rdfs:comment """An instructional unit that specifies planned learner engagement intended to support learning, practice, reflection, or the development of competences.

In OntoKSD, LearningActivity is a subclass of InstructionalUnit. It describes how learners are expected to engage with content, resources, other participants, or guided actions. It represents a pedagogical specification rather than an occurrence of the activity, actual learner participation, or demonstrated attainment.

A LearningActivity may organize explanation, exploration, discussion, experimentation, reflection, rehearsal, or guided problem solving. Examples include a guided discussion on ethical computing, collaborative exploration of automata, practice with recursion, and a review of software architecture alternatives.

LearningTask specializes LearningActivity by specifying a defined piece of learner work, with identifiable expected actions and outcomes or deliverables. LearningActivity also accommodates arrangements centered on exploration or interaction without requiring a task-like product. The distinction concerns instructional specificity, not whether one class represents a plan and the other an execution: both belong to the instructional specification layer.

A LearningActivity may be associated with competences through CompetenceActivation, situated through hasAcquisitionContext, and supported by EducationalResource instances through usesResource. Its objectives, expected engagement, and applicable conditions may also be documented independently of any competence association.

LearningActivity is distinct from Task, which identifies the reference performance addressed by a competence specification. Participating in a learning activity may provide opportunities to work toward that performance, but the activity and the competence-level task remain separately identifiable.

Within the adopted OntoKSD scope, LearningActivity represents the learning-oriented specification, whereas Assessment represents an evaluative specification. A learning activity may include feedback, self-reflection, or opportunities to observe performance without thereby constituting an Assessment. Where its work is assessed, the evaluative specification is represented separately and may be linked through isBasedOnActivity.

LearningActivity is also distinct from EducationalResource: the activity specifies expected engagement, while the resource is an artefact used to support it.

The class enables learning arrangements to be described and reused without asserting that they have been executed or that their intended learning outcomes have been achieved. Competence associations express pedagogical expectations; learner-specific participation, evidence, and judgments are represented separately.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningNeed
ontoksd:LearningNeed rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:comment """An explicitly recorded educational need concerning the development or improvement of a competence or competence constituent for an individual.

In OntoKSD, LearningNeed may be associated with a Profile through hasLearningNeed, whose inverse is identifiedIn. Its target may be specified through targetsCompetence or targetsCompetenceConstituent.

A learning need may express a documented difference between expected and assessed performance, or another educational concern identified through an explicit interpretation. Its description should state the basis, relevant context, and intended development target. Where proficiency levels are compared, their schemes and meanings must be compatible or their correspondence explicitly justified.

LearningNeed is distinct from a Competence specification, an assessment judgment, and an instructional recommendation. Identifying a need does not, by itself, select an activity, prescribe an intervention, or establish its effectiveness.

Absence of evidence, an unassessed constituent, or a low score alone does not automatically establish a learning need. The class records an identified need rather than asserting that the ontology has performed a diagnosis.

LearningNeed does not establish that intervention is mandatory or that the recorded need remains applicable indefinitely. Changes in its interpretation or status require explicit documentation."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningTask
ontoksd:LearningTask rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:LearningActivity ;
                     rdfs:comment """A learning activity that specifies a defined piece of learner work, including expected actions and identifiable outcomes or deliverables within an instructional context.

In OntoKSD, LearningTask is a subclass of LearningActivity and, consequently, of InstructionalUnit. It operationalizes planned learner engagement through an assignment, problem, exercise, case analysis, or other delimited work specification. It represents what learners are expected to do rather than an execution of that work, actual learner participation, or a completed product.

A LearningTask is distinguished from a more general LearningActivity by the specificity of its expected work and completion conditions. Its description may identify the problem to address, actions to perform, outcomes or deliverables to produce, resources to use, and applicable constraints. A deliverable need not be a persistent artefact: an explanation, demonstration, classification, or other observable response may constitute an expected outcome.

LearningTask must be distinguished from Task. Task belongs to the competence specification layer and expresses the reference performance that a competence is intended to enable. LearningTask belongs to the instructional layer and specifies work designed to provide opportunities to develop, practice, or demonstrate relevant competences. Neither class represents an execution event.

For example, a competence-level Task may concern constructing a model that recognizes a regular language. A LearningTask may require learners to construct a deterministic finite automaton for a specified language, use a designated simulator, submit test cases, and explain their modeling decisions. Different learning tasks may address the same reference performance under different pedagogical conditions, and one learning task may address multiple competences.

Competence associations are represented through CompetenceActivation. These associations qualify how each competence is expected to contribute to the learning task without replacing its complete instructional description. Expected actions, deliverables, and conditions must remain recordable even when no competence association has been asserted.

A LearningTask may use EducationalResource instances through usesResource and be associated with an AcquisitionContext through hasAcquisitionContext. Its specification should remain identifiable when reused; changes to its requirements or competence qualifications should be explicitly documented.

Examples include implementing an automaton for a given language, solving recursion exercises, modeling a database for a case study, debugging code and explaining the errors, or producing a comparison of alternative software designs.

LearningTask represents the learning-oriented work specification. Where that work is evaluated, the Assessment specification is represented separately and may be linked through isBasedOnActivity. Expected outputs remain distinct from collected evidence, and opportunities to demonstrate competence do not establish that a learner has attained it.""" ,
                                  """LearningTask
A subclass of LearningActivity representing a more concrete, bounded, and executable instructional unit in which learners are expected to perform a specific task in order to develop, practice, or demonstrate competencies.

In OntoKSD, LearningTask is used to model learning situations that are more explicitly goal-directed and operational than general learning activities. A learning task typically requires the learner to carry out a defined piece of work, solve a problem, produce an artefact, analyze a case, or complete a structured assignment within an instructional context.

As a subclass of LearningActivity, LearningTask inherits the general role of supporting competency-oriented learning. However, it is distinguished by its greater instructional specificity, clearer execution boundaries, and stronger association with concrete learner performance. In many cases, a learning task may also contribute to the production of evidence of competence, although its primary purpose remains instructional rather than purely evaluative.

Typical examples of LearningTask include:

implementing a deterministic finite automaton for a given language;

solving a set of programming problems involving recursion;

modeling a database schema for a case study;

classifying strings according to a formal grammar;

debugging a piece of source code and explaining the identified errors;

producing a report comparing alternative software design solutions;

completing a worksheet on logical propositions and truth tables.

Ontological rationale:
LearningTask is modeled as a subclass of LearningActivity because it represents a specialized form of learning-oriented instructional unit. While all learning tasks are learning activities, not all learning activities are sufficiently concrete, delimited, or task-oriented to qualify as learning tasks. This distinction allows OntoKSD to represent both broader pedagogical arrangements and more specific executable units within the instructional layer.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#OntoKSDRCD
ontoksd:OntoKSDRCD rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf rcd:RCD ,
                                   [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                     owl:onProperty ontoksd:representsCompetence ;
                                     owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                     owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                   ] .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Person
ontoksd:Person rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ProficiencyLevel
ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:comment """A qualitative category expressing a degree of performance according to an identifiable proficiency scheme.

In OntoKSD, ProficiencyLevel provides the categorical interpretation of assessed performance in relation to a Competence or CompetenceConstituent. Examples include Beginner, Basic, Intermediate, and Advanced. The meaning of each category depends on the descriptors, criteria, or numeric intervals established by its ProficiencyScheme.

A level may be assigned through interpretation of a numeric result using ScaleInterval or through explicitly documented qualitative descriptors. It is not itself a score, an evidence artefact, or a judgment record.

Learner-specific interpretations are associated with EvidencedCompetence or EvidencedCompetenceConstituent through hasProficiencyLevel. The same category may also express a curricular expectation through requiresMinimumProficiencyLevel. Expected and attributed proficiency remain distinguishable through their respective associations.

ProficiencyLevel is distinct from SkillLevel, which qualifies required performance within a competence specification. An attributed proficiency category does not automatically correspond to a Bloom level or another skill taxonomy.

Levels may be defined within a scheme through definesLevel, whose inverse is levelDefinedInScheme. An ordinal position may express their ordering within the applicable scheme, but ordering does not imply equal distances between categories or comparability across schemes. Identical labels in different schemes do not establish equivalent meanings.

A level assignment records an interpretation under specified conditions. It does not, by itself, establish complete mastery, progression over time, or transfer of performance to another context.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ProficiencyScheme
ontoksd:ProficiencyScheme rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                            owl:onProperty ontoksd:definesLevel ;
                                            owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                            owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                          ] ;
                          rdfs:comment """An interpretive scheme that defines proficiency categories and the criteria for assigning them to assessed performance.

In OntoKSD, ProficiencyScheme separates the meaning of proficiency classifications from the results and evidence being interpreted. It defines one or more ProficiencyLevel categories through definesLevel and documents how those categories apply to performance concerning a Competence or CompetenceConstituent.

A scheme may use numeric interpretation, qualitative descriptors, or an explicitly documented combination of both. A numeric scheme identifies its Scale through usesScale and its interpretive intervals through definesInterval. Each ScaleInterval specifies numeric boundaries and the corresponding proficiency category through correspondsToLevel. A qualitative scheme instead documents descriptors and criteria that distinguish its categories without requiring a numeric score.

Scale provides the numeric measurement range; ProficiencyScheme provides its interpretation. Different schemes may therefore use the same scale while adopting different boundaries, descriptors, or category meanings. Identical category labels do not establish equivalence between schemes.

An AssessmentOccurrence may identify an applicable scheme through usesProficiencyScheme. Where different judgments within an occurrence use different schemes, the scheme governing each interpretation must remain identifiable.

The scheme defines a basis for classification but does not, by itself, calculate a result, execute classification, or establish the validity of a judgment. Any automated procedure must be separately specified and evaluated.

A scheme may also provide the categories used to express curricular proficiency expectations. Its use must preserve the distinction between an expected level and a level attributed to an individual learner.

Descriptors, interval boundaries, and ordering should remain consistent with the scheme's documented meaning. Relevant revisions must be identifiable so that existing interpretations are not silently redefined.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Profile
ontoksd:Profile rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                  owl:onProperty ontoksd:isProfileOf ;
                                  owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                  owl:onClass ontoksd:Learner
                                ] ;
                rdfs:comment """An identifiable educational record that organizes competence-related information concerning a particular individual.

In OntoKSD, Profile is distinct from the person it describes, the reusable competence specifications, and the assessment participations through which individual performance is evaluated. Its owner is identified through isProfileOf, whose inverse is hasProfile.

A Profile may include EvidencedCompetence records through hasEvidencedCompetence, associated Evidence through hasEvidence, explicitly recorded LearningNeed instances through hasLearningNeed, and contextual role information where applicable.

The profile organizes records rather than asserting a single context-independent state of competence. Individual judgments retain their assessment occasions, supporting evidence, applicable criteria, proficiency interpretations, and relevant dates. Multiple records concerning the same competence must remain distinguishable rather than being silently replaced or combined.

Evidence included in a profile does not automatically support every judgment in that profile. Likewise, the profile owner must agree with the learner identified by the AssessmentParticipation grounding each associated evidential record. These cross-record correspondences require explicit verification.

A recorded LearningNeed identifies an educational concern or target documented for the individual. Its inclusion does not establish that the ontology has automatically diagnosed a competence gap. Absence of evidence is not, by itself, evidence of insufficient competence.

Profile supports retrieval of an individual's educational records without automatically establishing progression, aggregating judgments, recommending instruction, or certifying attainment. Such analyses require separately defined procedures and supporting information."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#RoleDetail
ontoksd:RoleDetail rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Scale
ontoksd:Scale rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:maxValue ;
                                owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                              ] ,
                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:minValue ;
                                owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                              ] ,
                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:step ;
                                owl:maxQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                              ] ;
              rdfs:comment """A numeric scoring structure that defines a bounded range of values for expressing assessment results.

In OntoKSD, Scale specifies exactly one minimum value through minValue and exactly one maximum value through maxValue. It may specify a positive increment through step when scores are restricted to discrete increments. Examples include scoring ranges from 0 to 10 or from 0 to 100.

Scale is distinct from an individual score, a ProficiencyLevel, and a ProficiencyScheme. It defines the numeric range; the scheme establishes how results are interpreted through intervals, descriptors, and proficiency categories. Different schemes may use the same scale without assigning identical meanings to its values.

A Scale may be associated with ScaleInterval instances through hasInterval, whose inverse is isIntervalOf. Interval boundaries and their inclusion or exclusion determine the numeric subranges used by an interpretive scheme.

The minimum must be lower than the maximum, interval boundaries must be compatible with the scale, and any recorded increment must be positive and have a documented application. These numeric conditions require explicit validation; cardinality restrictions alone do not enforce them.

A Scale does not automatically classify scores, normalize results from other scales, establish equal distances between proficiency categories, or make assessments comparable. Its interpretation must remain explicit in the applicable proficiency scheme and assessment specification.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ScaleInterval
ontoksd:ScaleInterval rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:correspondsToLevel ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onClass ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:isIntervalOf ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onClass ontoksd:Scale
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:includesLower ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:boolean
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:includesUpper ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:boolean
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:lowerBound ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                                      ] ,
                                      [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                        owl:onProperty ontoksd:upperBound ;
                                        owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                        owl:onDataRange xsd:decimal
                                      ] ;
                      rdfs:comment """A bounded numeric subrange of a Scale used to interpret assessment results within an identifiable ProficiencyScheme.

In OntoKSD, ScaleInterval specifies a lower bound through lowerBound, an upper bound through upperBound, and whether each endpoint is included through includesLower and includesUpper. Each endpoint and inclusion indicator has exactly one value.

An interval identifies its Scale through isIntervalOf and is associated with an interpretive scheme through intervalDefinedInScheme, whose inverse is definesInterval. Its corresponding ProficiencyLevel is identified through correspondsToLevel. These associations distinguish the numeric subrange from the proficiency meaning assigned to it.

For example, within a documented 0–10 scheme, [0.0, 3.0] may correspond to Beginner, while (8.0, 10.0] may correspond to Advanced. These boundaries and labels are scheme-specific rather than universal classifications.

Where the same numeric boundaries have different interpretations in different schemes, separate interval specifications should preserve the correspondence between each scheme and its assigned level.

Bounds must be ordered, remain within the associated scale, and define a non-empty interval. Overlaps, gaps, and boundary cases must be checked according to the scheme's intended coverage and classification policy. These conditions require explicit validation; recording bounds and cardinalities does not automatically enforce them.

ScaleInterval documents a numeric interpretation rule. It does not, by itself, classify a recorded score, assign proficiency to a learner, or establish competence attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Skill
ontoksd:Skill rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceConstituent ;
              rdfs:comment """A competence constituent that specifies a capability required for expected competent performance in a given domain.

In OntoKSD, Skill represents what a learner or practitioner is expected to be able to do, perform, or mobilize. It describes a capability within a competence specification, rather than asserting that a particular individual possesses or has demonstrated that capability.

Skills may concern cognitive, technical, interpersonal, or other action-oriented dimensions of performance. Examples include reasoning, problem solving, modeling, design, implementation, and communication. A Skill may be classified through hasSkillDimension and related to relevant Knowledge elements through appliesKnowledge.

Skill is distinct from Knowledge and Disposition. Knowledge identifies the content involved in expected performance, including facts, concepts, principles, and procedures. Skill specifies the capability to act upon or mobilize such content. Disposition identifies an orientation expected to shape how that capability is exercised. In particular, procedural knowledge describes a method or procedure, whereas Skill specifies the capability to perform or use it.

A Skill may be associated with a SkillLevel through hasSkillLevel when taxonomic qualification is relevant. SkillLevel identifies a classificatory level used to qualify performance; it does not itself denote an individuated skill. Such qualification is optional and does not define the identity of Skill.

Skill is also distinct from KnowledgeSkill. Skill specifies a capability as a constituent in its own right, whereas KnowledgeSkill pairs a Knowledge element with a required SkillLevel. These constructs support different forms of competence specification: explicitly identified capabilities and knowledge-specific performance requirements. A KnowledgeSkill pairing does not, by itself, identify a separate Skill constituent.

A Skill element may be associated with a Competence through hasSkill and reused across competence specifications. Where a performance level varies according to the competence or specification context, that requirement should be distinguished from a context-independent classification of the reusable Skill element.

Skill belongs to the specification layer. Learner-specific evidence concerning its demonstration is represented separately through EvidencedCompetenceConstituent and associated evidence and assessment relations. Including a Skill in a competence specification therefore expresses an expected capability, rather than evidence of individual attainment."""@en ;
              rdfs:label "Skill"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#SkillDimension
ontoksd:SkillDimension rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:comment """A classificatory category that identifies the capability dimension emphasized in the description of a Skill.

In OntoKSD, SkillDimension provides reusable controlled values associated with Skill through hasSkillDimension. It supports the organization and retrieval of explicitly specified capabilities according to their cognitive, technical, or interpersonal emphasis.

These dimensions are not inherently mutually exclusive. A skill may involve several dimensions, and multiple classifications may be assigned where their meanings are explicitly supported by the skill description. Classification depends on the capability described rather than solely on its label.

SkillDimension is distinct from SkillLevel, which qualifies required performance within a reference scheme, and from ProficiencyLevel, which expresses an interpretation of assessed performance. It also differs from ActivationMode, which characterizes expected engagement in a particular instructional association.

The dimensions constitute an organizational vocabulary adopted by OntoKSD rather than a claim that a curricular framework defines the same exhaustive taxonomy. Additional values may be introduced when their meanings and purposes are explicitly justified.

Assigning a dimension classifies the specified capability. It does not establish that a learner possesses or has demonstrated it.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#SpecificationPattern
ontoksd:SpecificationPattern rdf:type owl:Class ;
                             rdfs:comment """A classificatory category that identifies the structural pattern adopted for a competence specification, including its required constituent types and, where applicable, an explicit reference task.

In OntoKSD, SpecificationPattern distinguishes alternative ways of organizing the semantic specification of expected competent performance. Its instances are controlled values associated with Competence through hasSpecificationPattern.

OntoKSD supports four specification patterns:

• KSSpecificationPattern: requires Knowledge and Skill as explicitly represented constituents.

• KSASpecificationPattern: requires Knowledge, Skill, and Attitude as explicitly represented constituents. Attitude specializes Disposition, but an unspecified Disposition does not, by itself, satisfy the attitude requirement.

• CC2020SpecificationPattern: represents the KSD pattern through KnowledgeSkill pairings and Disposition constituents. Each pairing associates a Knowledge element with its required SkillLevel, preserving the correspondence between knowledge content and expected performance.

• CS2023SpecificationPattern: represents the KSD-T pattern through KnowledgeSkill pairings, Disposition constituents, and an explicitly represented Task associated directly with Competence through hasTask. Task is part of the specification structure but is not classified as a CompetenceConstituent.

SpecificationPattern is independent of whether a competence is atomic or composite. It identifies how the competence is specified, whereas structural composition describes whether and how it incorporates other competences. It also differs from a curricular framework alignment: adopting a specification pattern does not, by itself, establish correspondence with an externally defined competence.

The selected pattern identifies the minimum structural requirements applicable to the specification. Additional elements are not prohibited unless explicit restrictions establish such exclusions. Pattern-specific requirements are expressed through the corresponding competence classes and associated validation constraints; the pattern individual alone does not encode those requirements.

A pattern assignment classifies the declared form of a competence specification. It does not, by itself, demonstrate that all required information has been explicitly recorded, that the specification has been externally validated, or that an individual has demonstrated the competence.

SpecificationPattern therefore supports consistent authoring, interpretation, querying, and validation of competence specifications while preserving the distinctions between knowledge, skills, attitudes, dispositions, knowledge-specific performance requirements, and reference tasks.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#StructureType
ontoksd:StructureType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:comment """A classificatory category that identifies whether a competence specification is represented as atomic or composite in relation to other competence specifications.

In OntoKSD, instances of StructureType are controlled values associated with Competence through hasStructureType. Atomic identifies a specification without component competences, whereas Composite identifies a specification whose expected performance integrates component competences.

This classification concerns competence-to-competence organization. It is independent of SpecificationPattern, which identifies how knowledge, skills, attitudes, dispositions, knowledge–performance pairings, and reference tasks are organized within a competence specification. An atomic competence may therefore possess a detailed constituent structure, and a composite competence may adopt any supported specification pattern.

Atomicity and composition are interpreted within the scope and granularity of the represented specification. Atomicity does not imply that the performance is intrinsically indivisible or that no alternative specification could decompose it.

The controlled values Atomic and Composite correspond to the classes AtomicCompetence and CompositeCompetence through explicit class axioms. The absence of recorded component relations does not, by itself, establish atomicity.

StructureType classifies the organization of expected performance. It does not indicate learner attainment, proficiency, or whether component competences have been demonstrated in an integrated performance.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Task
ontoksd:Task rdf:type owl:Class ;
             rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                               owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasTaskStatement ;
                               owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                               owl:onDataRange rdf:langString
                             ] ;
             rdfs:comment """A specification-level entity that represents the task taken as reference for defining a competence.
    
In OntoKSD, Task is used to represent what a competence is expected to address, support, or enable in terms of purposeful performance. It belongs to the semantic structure of competence specification, rather than to the instructional context in which the competence may later be activated.
    
Task should not be confused with LearningTask, which denotes an instructional unit used for pedagogical enactment. A Task may be pedagogically operationalized through one or more instructional units, but it is not itself an instructional unit.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#TaskActivity
ontoksd:TaskActivity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:TaskElement ;
                     rdfs:comment """A TaskElement that identifies an action or operation required by a specification-level Task.

In OntoKSD, TaskActivity makes explicit what is to be done. It is associated with Task through hasTaskActivity and is commonly expressed by a verb or verb phrase in the task statement. Examples include designing, developing, documenting, evaluating, maintaining, improving, and researching.

For example, in “Develop a program for an open-ended problem,” developing is the task activity. In “Evaluate the efficiency of a parallel algorithm,” evaluating is the activity, while the algorithm is the referential target and the performance qualifications require separate analysis.

A Task may include multiple activities where its statement requires more than one action. Their association with the task does not, by itself, establish execution order, dependencies, or separate phases.

TaskActivity is distinct from TaskComponent, which identifies the action's referential target, and TaskConstraint, which identifies applicable qualifications or conditions. It is also distinct from LearningActivity, an instructional specification, and from an occurrence of an action.

TaskActivity does not itself denote a Skill or SkillLevel. An action named in a task statement does not automatically identify an individuated capability or determine its required taxonomic level. Such correspondences require explicit specification and justification.

The class represents required action within the task description rather than the form of engagement assigned to a particular CompetenceActivation through ActivationMode. Its inclusion records expected performance, not evidence that a learner has performed it.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#TaskComponent
ontoksd:TaskComponent rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:TaskElement ;
                      rdfs:comment """A TaskElement that identifies the referential target or subject matter of an action required by a specification-level Task.

In OntoKSD, TaskComponent makes explicit what the task concerns or acts upon. It is associated with Task through hasTaskComponent and is commonly expressed by a noun or noun phrase in the task statement. Examples include a program, algorithm, language, architecture, dataset, service, or platform.

In “Develop a program for an open-ended problem,” the program is a target of the required action. In “Evaluate the efficiency of a parallel algorithm,” the algorithm identifies the principal entity under examination. The analysis should preserve relevant wording, including a property or aspect that is itself the focus of evaluation.

A Task may reference multiple components. Their association does not, by itself, identify which activity concerns each component when the statement contains several actions and targets. Such correspondences should remain explicit in the task description where needed.

TaskComponent represents a referential element of the specification, not necessarily a particular existing artefact. A program to be developed, for example, is an expected target of work rather than evidence that a completed program exists.

The class is distinct from TaskActivity, which identifies the required action, and TaskConstraint, which identifies applicable qualifications or conditions. It is also distinct from Knowledge: naming an algorithm or architecture as a task target does not automatically establish which knowledge elements are required.

The term component denotes an element of task analysis. It does not assert physical parthood, competence composition, or membership in CompetenceConstituent. Its inclusion records the intended focus of performance rather than an observed outcome.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#TaskConstraint
ontoksd:TaskConstraint rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:TaskElement ;
                       rdfs:comment """A TaskElement that specifies a condition, limitation, requirement, or qualification delimiting the performance addressed by a specification-level Task.

In OntoKSD, TaskConstraint is associated with Task through hasTaskConstraint. It identifies conditions relevant to interpreting the required action and its referential target. Such conditions may be expressed by adjectives, adverbs, or qualifying phrases, but their classification depends on meaning rather than grammatical form alone.

Constraints may concern the problem, such as its scale or open-ended nature; the expected solution, such as required security, reliability, or cost limits; or the implementation, such as parallel or distributed execution. In “Develop a program for an open-ended problem,” open-ended qualifies the problem addressed by the task.

A property mentioned in a task statement is not automatically a constraint. In “Evaluate the efficiency of a parallel algorithm,” efficiency may identify the aspect to be evaluated rather than a required degree of efficiency, while parallel qualifies the algorithm under consideration. A requirement such as “produce a solution within the specified execution-time limit” states a performance constraint more explicitly.

TaskConstraint is distinct from TaskActivity, which identifies what is to be done, and TaskComponent, which identifies the referential target. It also differs from ActivationConstraint, which qualifies a particular competence–instructional unit association, and from an assessment criterion used to judge performance. Correspondences between these elements require explicit interpretation rather than automatic identification.

The condition should be described sufficiently to support consistent interpretation, including relevant limits or applicability where stated. Multiple constraints may apply to the same task without implying that their compatibility or satisfaction has been verified.

TaskConstraint belongs to the specification layer. Recording it does not establish that the task has been performed, that the condition has been satisfied, or that an automated compliance check exists.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#TaskElement
ontoksd:TaskElement rdf:type owl:Class ;
                    rdfs:comment """An analytically identifiable element of a specification-level Task description.

In OntoKSD, TaskElement provides a common superclass for TaskActivity, TaskComponent, and TaskConstraint. These subclasses represent complementary aspects of task meaning:

• TaskActivity identifies the action or operation to be performed.

• TaskComponent identifies the object, artefact, or other referential target of that action.

• TaskConstraint identifies a condition or qualification delimiting the expected performance.

A Task may be associated with these elements through hasTaskElement and its specialized properties hasTaskActivity, hasTaskComponent, and hasTaskConstraint. This analytical decomposition is optional and does not require every task statement to contain an explicitly represented element of each type.

The structure supports task analysis informed by CS2023 and traceability within the Competency Specification Process. Where elements are derived from source wording, their correspondence and any interpretive decisions should remain explicit. Recording an element does not establish that it was automatically extracted or logically inferred.

TaskElement belongs to the competence specification layer. It is distinct from CompetenceConstituent, which organizes knowledge, skills, dispositions, and knowledge–performance pairings, and from InstructionalUnit, which specifies planned pedagogical engagement.

A TaskConstraint qualifies the reference task, whereas an ActivationConstraint qualifies a competence's association with a particular instructional unit. Similar wording may occur at both levels, but neither association is automatically implied by the other.

TaskElement supports the explicit representation of task meaning without replacing the complete task statement or asserting that the expected performance has occurred.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd/rcd#RCD
rcd:RCD rdf:type owl:Class ;
        rdfs:comment """An identifiable record describing a reusable competency definition for reference, publication, and exchange.

In the OntoKSD integration, RCD represents a documentary view of a Competence specification. It provides an identifier and a human-readable title, with optional description, structured definition, and metadata. It is distinct from the competence specification itself, instructional associations, learner profiles, supporting evidence, and assessment judgments.

The record is linked to its internal specification through representsCompetence, whose inverse is hasRCD. The mapping profile identifies which OntoKSD information is included, summarized, or referenced externally.

The core record supports identification and descriptive exchange. Preservation of knowledge–performance pairings, task structure, composition, specialization, and other domain relationships requires explicit mapping conventions and access to the corresponding structured information. A textual summary alone does not preserve all OntoKSD semantics.

This ontology provides an OWL representation oriented to the IEEE 1484.20.1 RCD data model. It is an application-defined representation, not an assertion that IEEE specifies this RDF vocabulary or that every instance conforms to the standard.

Required data presence, identifier uniqueness, serialization rules, and other exchange constraints require separate validation. OWL cardinalities do not ensure that all mandatory fields are explicitly recorded.

An RCD record does not establish semantic equivalence with another definition, interoperability with every receiving system, regulatory compliance, or learner attainment.""" ,
                     """OntoKSD–RCD Mapping Profile

The mapping produces an identifiable documentary representation of an OntoKSD competence specification using the RCD vocabulary.

The minimum exchange record contains an explicit identifier and at least one language-tagged title. Descriptions, structured statements, and metadata are included according to the information available and the declared exchange scope.

Structured statements distinguish specification patterns, constituents, knowledge-specific performance requirements, reference tasks, composition, and specialization. The mapping preserves the association between each knowledge element and its required skill level rather than exporting them as unrelated lists.

The RCD record remains linked to the OntoKSD specification it represents. Where domain relationships are summarized rather than encoded completely, the corresponding structured specification must remain identifiable and accessible under the publication policy.

The mapping excludes learner-specific evidence, assessment results, proficiency judgments, and learning needs. Referencing these records requires a separate exchange profile.

The profile supports identification, descriptive exchange, and traceability. It does not claim that an RCD record alone enables complete reconstruction of the OntoKSD graph. Standard conformance, export correctness, and semantic preservation are evaluated separately.""" .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept
skos:Concept rdf:type owl:Class .


#################################################################
#    Individuals
#################################################################

###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Abstract
ontoksd:Abstract rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AcessmentProject
ontoksd:AcessmentProject rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                         rdfs:label "Acessment Project" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentAgentTypeScheme
ontoksd:AssessmentAgentTypeScheme rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                           skos:ConceptScheme .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentModalityScheme
ontoksd:AssessmentModalityScheme rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                          skos:ConceptScheme .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentPresentation
ontoksd:AssessmentPresentation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                               rdfs:label "Presentation" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentStrategyScheme
ontoksd:AssessmentStrategyScheme rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                          skos:ConceptScheme .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Assessor
ontoksd:Assessor rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role> .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Atomic
ontoksd:Atomic rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        ontoksd:StructureType ;
               owl:differentFrom ontoksd:Composite ;
               rdfs:comment """Atomic

A controlled StructureType value identifying a competence specification represented without component competences.

When associated with a Competence through hasStructureType, Atomic classifies that specification as AtomicCompetence according to the corresponding class definition. The classification excludes decomposition through hasComponentCompetence while allowing knowledge, skill, dispositional, and knowledge–performance constituents.

Atomicity applies to the represented specification at its adopted granularity. It does not assert intrinsic indivisibility and must not be inferred solely from the absence of recorded components.

Atomic is distinct from Composite.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BroaderThanExternalAlignment
ontoksd:BroaderThanExternalAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                              ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                                     rdfs:comment """BroaderThanExternalAlignment

An alignment classification asserting that the internal Competence encompasses the performance scope of the ExternalCompetence and includes additional substantive requirements.

The direction is internal to external: the internal specification is broader. The justification must identify the external requirements encompassed and the additional scope of the internal specification.

Use this type only when scope inclusion is supported. Additional wording, more knowledge elements, or a higher taxonomic level does not, by itself, establish broader coverage. If the specifications overlap without inclusion, use PartialAlignment.

This value does not create a composition, specialization, or prerequisite relationship, nor establish that a learner's judgment transfers between specifications.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CC2020SpecificationPattern
ontoksd:CC2020SpecificationPattern rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                            ontoksd:SpecificationPattern ;
                                   owl:differentFrom ontoksd:CS2023SpecificationPattern ,
                                                     ontoksd:KSASpecificationPattern ,
                                                     ontoksd:KSSpecificationPattern ;
                                   rdfs:comment """CC2020SpecificationPattern

A specification pattern in which a competence is represented according to the competency-oriented formulation adopted in ACM/IEEE CC2020. In this pattern, a competence is specified through a competency statement supported by a structured enumeration of knowledge, skills, and dispositions, where the skill dimension is operationalized through explicit KnowledgeSkill pairings that associate knowledge elements with required skill-level values.

This pattern captures the CC2020 view that competence is structured through the integration of knowledge, skills, and dispositions in the performance of a task. In OntoKSD, however, the task component may remain either explicitly represented or implicitly embedded in the competency statement, reflecting the way CC2020 commonly presents competency specifications.

CC2020SpecificationPattern therefore enables OntoKSD to represent competencies whose internal structure is centered on Knowledge × SkillLevel pairings, rather than on fully individuated Skill constituents alone, while preserving the dispositional dimension as an integral part of the specification.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CS2023SpecificationPattern
ontoksd:CS2023SpecificationPattern rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                            ontoksd:SpecificationPattern ;
                                   owl:differentFrom ontoksd:KSASpecificationPattern ,
                                                     ontoksd:KSSpecificationPattern ;
                                   rdfs:comment """CS2023SpecificationPattern

A specification pattern in which a competence is represented according to the competency framework adopted in \\textit{Computer Science Curricula 2023} (CS2023). In this pattern, a competence is specified through an explicit task statement, a competency statement, the required knowledge elements, the required skill levels, and the relevant professional dispositions.

Unlike CC2020-style formulations, in which the task context may remain implicit within the competency statement, CS2023SpecificationPattern makes the task an explicit structural component of the specification. The task expresses, in broad and generally non-technical terms, what is to be accomplished, while the competency statement expresses, in more technical terms, how a graduate is expected to approach or perform that task.

This pattern enables OntoKSD to represent competencies in a way that is consistent with the CS2023 effort to make competency specifications more operational, analyzable, and closely aligned with curricular design. It is especially suitable for competency descriptions in which task context, competency statement, knowledge requirements, skill expectations, and dispositions are deliberately separated and explicitly structured.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CloseAlignment
ontoksd:CloseAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                       rdfs:comment """An alignment classification indicating substantial similarity in intended performance and semantic coverage while acknowledging differences that prevent an equivalence claim.

Use this type when the specifications are sufficiently comparable to support a documented correspondence, but differ in aspects such as emphasis, formulation, conditions, or explicitness. The justification must identify both the substantial common ground and the remaining differences.

Do not use this type merely because the specifications share terminology. Where the correspondence is better characterized by scope inclusion or limited overlap, use the appropriate broader, narrower, or partial type.

Close correspondence does not establish equivalence, substitution, or complete coverage.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Composite
ontoksd:Composite rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           ontoksd:StructureType ;
                  rdfs:comment """Composite

A controlled StructureType value identifying a competence specification represented through the integration of two or more component competences.

When associated with a Competence through hasStructureType, Composite classifies that specification as CompositeCompetence according to the corresponding class definition. Its direct components are identified through hasComponentCompetence, and their intended integration must be documented.

Composite denotes an integrated performance specification rather than an arbitrary grouping of competences. Its assignment does not establish that all required component descriptions have been explicitly recorded or that a learner has demonstrated the integrated performance.

Composite is distinct from Atomic.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ContextualAlignment
ontoksd:ContextualAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                     ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                            rdfs:comment """An alignment classification indicating a correspondence whose relevance depends on explicitly identified contextual circumstances.

Use this type when a particular domain, professional setting, instructional purpose, or application scenario provides the principal basis for relating the specifications. The justification must identify those circumstances, the relevant correspondence, and its limits.

Mere co-occurrence in the same activity, course, or document is insufficient. If context only qualifies an otherwise clear semantic relationship, record it in the justification and select the more informative semantic type.

ContextualAlignment does not establish general correspondence outside the stated circumstances, semantic equivalence, or curricular coverage.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Course
ontoksd:Course rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Kind> .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CurricularContinuityAlignment
ontoksd:CurricularContinuityAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                               ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                                      rdfs:comment """An alignment classification indicating a correspondence whose relevance depends on explicitly identified contextual circumstances.

Use this type when a particular domain, professional setting, instructional purpose, or application scenario provides the principal basis for relating the specifications. The justification must identify those circumstances, the relevant correspondence, and its limits.

Mere co-occurrence in the same activity, course, or document is insufficient. If context only qualifies an otherwise clear semantic relationship, record it in the justification and select the more informative semantic type.

ContextualAlignment does not establish general correspondence outside the stated circumstances, semantic equivalence, or curricular coverage.

CurricularContinuityAlignment

An alignment classification indicating that the ExternalCompetence provides an explicitly justified curricular antecedent or developmental reference for the internal Competence.

Use this type to document continuity across educational stages or curricular expectations when the relationship concerns preparation or development rather than equivalent performance. The justification must explain what continuity is proposed and which additional demands distinguish the internal specification.

An earlier educational stage alone is insufficient. The relationship does not assert that the external competence is a mandatory prerequisite, that every learner has attained it, or that individual progression has been demonstrated.

This value does not establish equivalence, automatic transfer of proficiency, or complete curricular articulation.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EquivalentAlignment
ontoksd:EquivalentAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                     ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                            rdfs:comment """An alignment classification asserting equivalent intended performance and semantic coverage between an internal Competence and an ExternalCompetence within an explicitly delimited comparison scope.

Use this type only when the documented comparison identifies no material difference in the requirements relevant to that scope, including performance, subject matter, conditions, and dispositional expectations where applicable. Similar wording or a shared topic is insufficient.

If equivalence concerns only a selected aspect of a broader external statement, use a restricted or partial correspondence instead. The justification must identify the compared specifications and the basis for the equivalence claim.

This value does not establish identity, logical class equivalence, interchangeability outside the stated scope, or equivalent learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Exam
ontoksd:Exam rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                      ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#GeneralFrameworkAlignment
ontoksd:GeneralFrameworkAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                           ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                                  rdfs:comment """An alignment classification indicating that an internal Competence contributes to the educational intent of a broad external competence or framework-level expectation.

Use this type when the external statement provides an overarching curricular orientation and the internal competence offers an identifiable contribution without claiming fine-grained equivalence or a demonstrated scope-inclusion relationship. The justification must identify the contribution and the external aspects it addresses.

Shared aspirational language is insufficient. Where the comparison supports a more precise semantic relationship, use that type instead.

This value does not establish full coverage of the external expectation, satisfaction of a framework as a whole, or regulatory compliance.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#HighReliability
ontoksd:HighReliability rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Instructional
ontoksd:Instructional rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KSASpecificationPattern
ontoksd:KSASpecificationPattern rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                         ontoksd:SpecificationPattern ;
                                owl:differentFrom ontoksd:KSSpecificationPattern ;
                                rdfs:comment """KSASpecificationPattern

A specification pattern in which a competence is represented through explicitly individuated Knowledge, Skill, and Attitude constituents.

This pattern corresponds to formulations commonly found in the competency literature in which the internal structure of a competence is expressed through separate knowledge, skill, and attitude elements, each modeled as a distinct competence constituent. Unlike patterns centered on KnowledgeSkill pairings, KSASpecificationPattern preserves Skill as an explicit constituent in its own right, thereby supporting representations in which knowledge, skill, and disposition are independently identified and semantically related within the competence specification.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KSSpecificationPattern
ontoksd:KSSpecificationPattern rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        ontoksd:SpecificationPattern .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Learner
ontoksd:Learner rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                         <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role> .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LowReliability
ontoksd:LowReliability rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#MediumReliability
ontoksd:MediumReliability rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#NarrowerThanExternalAlignment
ontoksd:NarrowerThanExternalAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                               ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                                      rdfs:comment """An alignment classification asserting that the internal Competence addresses a restricted portion of the performance scope of the ExternalCompetence.

The direction is internal to external: the internal specification is narrower. The justification must identify the portion addressed and the external requirements that remain outside the internal scope.

Use this type only when the internal performance requirements can be situated within the external scope. A lower taxonomic level or a more detailed formulation does not, by itself, establish this relationship.

The alignment does not assert complete coverage of the external competence, formal specialization, or equivalence of learner attainment.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Observed
ontoksd:Observed rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#OralAssessmentModalityType
ontoksd:OralAssessmentModalityType rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                            skos:Concept ;
                                   skos:inScheme ontoksd:AssessmentModalityScheme .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#PartialAlignment
ontoksd:PartialAlignment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  ontoksd:AlignmentType ;
                         rdfs:comment """An alignment classification indicating a substantive but limited overlap between an internal Competence and an ExternalCompetence.

Use this type when only identifiable aspects correspond and the relationship is not adequately described as complete scope inclusion in either direction. The justification must state what corresponds and which relevant aspects remain unmatched.

A shared keyword or broad theme is insufficient. Where one specification fully falls within the other's performance scope, use a directional broader or narrower classification instead.

PartialAlignment does not establish complete coverage of either specification or justify transferring judgments between them.""" .


